var WK_KOREA = [
{"id":"korea-bulgogi","name":"Bulgogi","nameAlt":"Marinated Beef — the word means 'fire meat'","aliases":["bulgogi","korean bbq beef","sobulgogi"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","grill","bbq","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","bbq"],"ingredients":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain · 20ml soy sauce · 12ml pear or apple juice (the tenderiser that is not a secret) · 10ml mirin or dry sherry · 10ml sesame oil · 5g brown sugar · 8g garlic, minced · 3g ginger, grated · 2 spring onions, chopped · a pinch of black pepper","method":"THIN SLICES ARE THE DISH. Bulgogi means 'fire meat' and its entire character is built on slices so thin they cook in seconds on a screaming-hot pan — about 3mm, cut across the grain. Part-freeze the beef for 30 minutes and it slices like butter. NO SHEET OF BEEF SHOULD BE THICKER THAN A TOASTED SANDWICH.\n\nTHE MARINADE IS THE TWIST AND THE PEAR IS THE TRICK. Whisk the soy, pear juice, mirin, sesame oil, brown sugar, garlic, ginger and pepper. The pear is doing real chemistry: its enzymes (and apple's, its nearest shelf cousin) tenderise the meat — and its sweetness caramelises into that lacquered, savoury-sweet edge. This is the difference between Korean barbecue and plain grilled meat.\n\nMARINATE 30 MINUTES TO OVERNIGHT. Thirty minutes is the floor, overnight is the peak — the pear's enzymes need time to work their way in, and the soy deepens. Do not salt the beef first: the marinade is the seasoning.\n\nSEAR FAST, IN TWO BATCHES, IN A SMOKING PAN. Get a pan or griddle ripping hot and sear the slices in a single layer, 30–45 seconds a side, until the edges catch and caramelise. Crowd the pan and the beef steams grey — space the slices and it lacquers. Two batches are always better than one crowded one.\n\nSERVE STRAIGHT OFF THE PAN, SCATTERED WITH THE RESERVED SPRING ONION. Bulgogi waits for no one — the contrast of hot caramelised edges against cool rice and sharp kimchi is the whole plate. Leftover marinade, boiled 3 minutes in a pan, is the classic dipping sauce.","cookTime":"Prep 15–20 min · Marinate 30 min–overnight · Cook 3–4 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 36g · Carbs 16g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Marinated raw beef keeps 2 days in the fridge. Cooked bulgogi keeps 3 days and is superb cold — and famously folds into tomorrow's fried rice. Freezes well raw, in the marinade, for a month.","chefNotes":"The pear is not optional decoration — its enzymes do the tenderising that makes bulgogi silk instead of steak. Slice thin, marinate long, and sear in batches over a roaring pan. The caramelised, lacquered edges are the point.","pairsWith":"Steamed white rice · Kimchi · A bowl of sliced cucumber in vinegar · Cold beer","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bulgogi's name literally means 'fire meat' — and it has been cooked over open flame since the days of the Goguryeo kingdom, around 2,000 years ago. The sweet lacquered soy marinade is the modern chapter: it only became the bulgogi the world knows in the 20th century, when the sesame-oil-and-pear glaze perfected what the fire had started.","howThisFeels":"The hiss as the thin slices hit a screaming-hot pan, the buttery-sweet smoke rising, and then the first caramelised bite against cold rice — the whole table suddenly very quiet.","leftovers":["Chop leftover bulgogi small and fold it into hot rice with a spoon of gochujang and sesame — bulgogi fried rice, the family's favourite day-two use.","Wrap cold bulgogi with lettuce and a smear of gochujang for ssam — a crisp, spicy hand roll that might be the best version of all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken Bulgogi (Dak Bulgogi)","icon":"🐔","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same pear-and-soy lacquer on chicken thigh — juicier than breast, and it takes the glaze even better.","trivia":"Dak bulgogi uses the same marinade with chicken thighs — and thighs are the right call: the extra fat keeps the fast-seared slices silky where breast would dry out in the same 45 seconds.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g chicken thighs, sliced thin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Chicken must cook through, so give it a minute per side rather than 45 seconds, and let it rest one minute before serving. The pear marinade works exactly the same — the enzymes don't know what kind of meat they're tenderising."}]}},{"name":"Pork Bulgogi (Dweji Bulgogi)","icon":"🐖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same lacquer on pork — richer, and the most common street-version of Korean BBQ outside Korea itself.","trivia":"Dweji bulgogi swaps in pork shoulder, and it is the bulgogi you are most likely to meet from a Korean street stall — pork is cheaper than beef, the marinade loves it, and the caramelisation is even faster.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g pork shoulder, sliced thin against the grain"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Pork shoulder wants a touch more time than beef — sear a full 90 seconds per side so it's cooked through — and skip the resting hang-up: pork is short-shrift if you prefer it well done."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-bibimbap","name":"Bibimbap","nameAlt":"The 'mixed rice' bowl — every colour of the Korean table in one bowl","aliases":["bibimbap","korean mixed rice","dolsot bibimbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","bowl","vegetables","egg"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox"],"ingredients":"80g cooked rice · 40g beef mince (optional) · 1 egg · 30g spinach · 30g carrot (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g courgette (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g bean sprouts · 15g sesame oil · 10ml soy sauce · 5g gochujang (the red paste that makes it bibimbap) · 1 clove garlic · a pinch of sugar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"BIBIMBAP MEANS 'MIXED RICE' AND THE MIX IS THE MEAL. Line up the vegetables, cook each in its own oil, and pile them around the rice in the national colours: green spinach, orange carrot, pale sprouts, white rice, and the red gochujang on top. The bowl should look like a small garden before it is stirred.\n\n⚖️ EVERY VEGETABLE IS SEASONED, NOT JUST COOKED. Each one gets its own minute in the pan with a whisper of salt and, ideally, a drop of sesame oil: spinach wilted and seasoned with soy and garlic; carrot and courgette stir-fried separately so their colours and textures stay distinct. Seasoned separately is the difference between bibimbap and a rice bowl with vegetables on it.\n\nTHE EGG IS THE LAST ACT — FROM THE PAN, STRAIGHT TO THE TOP. Fry it sunny-side up with a soft, runny yolk — the yolk is the sauce of the second half of this dish, the one that binds the mix when you stir at the table.\n\nIF YOU HAVE BEEF, BROWN IT HARD FIRST. Season the mince and cook it in a hot pan until properly brown — then reserve a spoon of its pan juice to stir into the finished bowl.\n\nNOW THE MOVEMENT THE DISH IS NAMED FOR: STIR. The runny yolk, the gochujang, the sesame oil and every separate flavour are folded into the rice with chopsticks until every grain is glossy and red — bibimbap is a dish you complete at the table, not on the stove. Scatter toasted sesame seeds over the stirred bowl.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Carbs 68g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best prepared and stirred fresh. The cooked rice and vegetables keep separately 2 days in the fridge — the vegetables re-warm well in a pan; the rice in the microwave. The dish does not freeze well (the vegetables weep on thawing).","chefNotes":"Season each vegetable in its own pan minute — that is the whole textural argument of the bowl. Get real gochujang (Korean chilli paste) if you can find it; it is the red heart of the mix. And never stir it on the stove: the stirring IS the ritual, done at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of soup (clear or kimchi) on the side · Cold barley or green tea","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bibimbap exists because of Korean jesa — the ancestor memorial rites that once laid out many small dishes in a specific order. Leftovers and small portions were mixed into rice so nothing was wasted, and the 'mixed rice' became so beloved it shed its leftovers-only history entirely. In Jeonju, the dish's spiritual home, the version is famously luxurious, topped with raw beef and egg yolk.","howThisFeels":"A bowl of colour that looks almost too pretty to stir — then the runny yolk, the gochujang and the sesame oil are folded in and it becomes the happiest thing in the kitchen.","leftovers":["The next-day move is kimchi fried rice — chop any leftover vegetables and the beef into a hot pan with the cold rice and a spoon of gochujang, and fry it until it crackles.","Leftover rice and vegetables make a perfect lunchbox bibimbap — keep the gochujang in its own little tub and stir it in at the table."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Dolsot (Sizzling Stone Bowl)","icon":"🔥","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The restaurant version with a soundtrack — served in a screaming-hot stone bowl that crackles when the rice touches it, and leaves a crust of crispy golden rice at the bottom.","trivia":"Dolsot bibimbap arrives in a stone bowl heated until smoking — the rice that touches the hot stone fries into a crispy golden crust (nurungji) that is half the pleasure. You can fake most of the experience with a cast-iron pan or a screaming-hot ovenproof bowl: heat the dish and a spoon of sesame oil in it, then build the bibimbap inside.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"sesame oil, for the hot bowl"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Heat the stone bowl or a small cast-iron pan over high heat with a teaspoon of sesame oil until it shimmers. Build the rice and toppings directly in the hot bowl — the rice will crackle and crisp on the bottom as it cooks. Serve sizzling, with the warning that the bowl is dangerously hot."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"Every colour of the bibimbap garden, no meat — with mushrooms stepping in for the beef's savour.","trivia":"A vegetarian bibimbap is never a thin version in Korea: the dish is fundamentally a mixture of seasoned vegetables, and a good shiitake or shimeji mushroom, browned hard, closes most of the gap the mince would leave.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef mince (optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g mushrooms, sliced and browned"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the sliced mushrooms hard in a hot dry pan — no oil for the first minute so they release water — then add a drop of soy and sesame at the end. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. For a fully vegan bowl, finish with a spoon of crispy fried tofu in place of the egg."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-fried-rice","name":"Kimchi Fried Rice","nameAlt":"Kimchi Bokkeumbap — Korea's favourite fridge-clearing pan","aliases":["kimchi bokkeumbap","kimchi fried rice","bokkeumbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","stir-fry","quick","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","quick","lunchbox","bachelor"],"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional, for extra heat) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced · a splash of vegetable oil","method":"COLD RICE, NOT FRESH. Day-old rice from the fridge fries loose and separate; fresh rice is swollen with steam and turns to porridge in the pan. No day-old rice? Spread fresh rice on a tray and freeze it 20 minutes to dry it out. THE COLD RICE IS THE WHOLE FIRST RULE.\n\nFRY THE KIMCHI HARD FIRST — THIS IS THE DISH'S SECRET SOUL. Chop the kimchi, squeeze out its juice and keep the juice, then fry the chopped kimchi in a hot pan with the oil until the edges catch and it darkens — usually 3 minutes. Raw kimchi tastes raw; fried kimchi turns sweet, sour and deeply savoury. That 3 minutes of browning is the entire difference between kimchi fried rice and rice with kimchi stirred in.\n\nADD THE GOCHUJANG AND THE RICE AND SMASH THE SAUCE INTO IT. Push the fried kimchi to one side, drop in the gochujang and let it sizzle in the oil, then the rice, and press everything flat with the spatula — let the rice toast against the hot pan for 30 seconds before you toss it, so the grains catch golden. WHICH IS ALSO THE WOK HEI OF KIMCHI FRIED RICE.\n\nSEASON AND TOSS. Splash in the soy, the sesame oil, the sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice — the juice recreates the fermented tang that frying was cooking off. Toss until every grain is glossy and red.\n\nCROWN WITH A FRIED EGG. Sunny-side-up, runny yolk — the yolk is the second sauce of the dish, broken at the table and stirred into the hot rice. Scatter spring onion and sesame seeds on top.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 66g · Fat 17g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best the minute it's fried. Keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats without shame in a hot pan with a splash of water. Freezes acceptably for a month — though the egg never does (add it fresh).","chefNotes":"Fry the chopped kimchi until the edges catch — that browning is the whole move. Use day-old rice, let it toast in the pan for 30 seconds before tossing, and never skip the fried egg on top: the runny yolk is half the sauce.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang soup · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Bulgogi","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi fried rice was born as the ultimate fridge-clearing dish — the last-of-the-pantry move Korean students and bachelors perfected. It got so beloved that dedicated kimchi fried rice restaurants exist across Korea, serving bowls with a matching fried egg as the default, and it is regularly voted Korean comfort food.","howThisFeels":"The sharp fermented smell of kimchi hitting a screaming-hot pan, the edges catching and darkening — and then a red, glossy bowl of rice with a fried egg winking at you from the top.","leftovers":["Fold a slice of cheese into the hot rice with the lid on and let it melt — cheese kimchi fried rice is a legitimate Korean favourite, salty-tangy and unholy-good.","Wrap it in a sheet of nori with the fried egg and a little gochujang for a kimbap-style hand roll the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The pantry hero — old rice, older kimchi, one egg, and the whole pan smells like Seoul.","trivia":"The classic is the fridge-clearing original: whatever the crisper had is welcome, but the kimchi and the fried egg are the law. In Korea this is the bachelor's dish, the student's dish, and the midnight snack that outranks takeaway.","delta":{},"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced","method":["Fry the chopped kimchi hard until its edges darken, 3 minutes.","Push aside, sizzle the gochujang in the oil, add the cold rice and let it toast 30 seconds before tossing.","Season with soy, sesame oil, sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice.","Crown with the fried egg and spring onion. Stir the yolk into the rice at the table."]},{"name":"Bacon & Cheese","icon":"🥓","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The Western-Korean cross that works — smoky bacon and a molten cheese crust over the tangy rice, a heart attack you will happily eat twice.","trivia":"Bacon and cheese are the two great Western additions to kimchi fried rice — cheese because its fat cuts the sharp kimchi acid beautifully, bacon because its smoke plays against the ferment like it was born to. Both are now standard-issue in Seoul's fried-rice bars.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g bacon, chopped"},{"item":"20g mozzarella or cheddar, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the chopped bacon first, crisp it, and use its rendered fat in place of the oil for the kimchi — the kimchi fries in bacon fat, which is exactly as good as it sounds. After the rice is fried and seasoned, press it flat, scatter the cheese over, cover the pan for 1 minute until it melts into a molten crust, and eat straight from the pan."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-tteokbokki","name":"Tteokbokki","nameAlt":"The beloved street-food rice cakes in a spicy-sweet red sauce","aliases":["tteokbokki","spicy rice cakes","topokki"],"course":"main","type":["main","street-food","rice-cake","quick"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","quick","snack"],"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes (tteok – fresh or frozen) · 20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang (Korean chilli paste) · 8g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or mild red chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or brown sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":"THE RICE CAKES ARE THE WHOLE TEXTURE. Tteokbokki is all about glossy, chewy cylinders of rice cake — cut them if frozen, and if they are fresh and soft, no soaking needed. If frozen, soak in cold water 10 minutes first so they heat through without going rubbery. THEY SHOULD BE SO CHEWY THEY ALMOST SQUEAK ON YOUR TEETH.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS THE SOUL AND IT IS BUILT IN THE PAN. Combine the water (or anchovy-kelp stock for depth), gochujang, gochugaru, soy sauce and honey in the pan — this is a sauce that comes together in the pan, not in a bowl first, because the simmering is what thickens and blends it.\n\n⚖️ THE TRUE STOCK IS THE SECRET. Anchovy-kelp stock (dashima stock) is what the street vendors use, and it is the difference between tteokbokki and noodles in a chilli packet. A 5cm piece of dried kelp simmered in the water for 5 minutes, then removed, is enough to change the whole bowl — the shop shortcut is a splash of good broth or a scrap of kombu.\n\nBRING TO A BUBBLE, THEN ADD THE RICE CAKES. Simmer over a medium flame, stirring gently, for 8–10 minutes — the sauce reduces, thickens and clings to the cakes. Gently is the word: rice cakes are fragile and sticky, and a hard stir breaks them into mush. Add the fish cakes and boiled egg for the last 3 minutes, and the spring onion off the heat.\n\nSERVE INSTANTLY, WITH A SPOONFUL OF THE THICK SAUCE OVER EVERYTHING. The chewy rice cakes carry the sweet-spicy sauce like nothing else — and the classic finish is to mop the last sauce with a piece of fried (or even plain) gimbap.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 12 min","kcal":"~430 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 80g · Fat 6g · Sodium ~950mg","storage":"Best eaten immediately — rice cakes harden and turn off as they cool. Keeps a day in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water to loosen the sauce. Does not freeze (the texture is everything).","chefNotes":"Chewy rice cakes and a glossy sweet-spicy sauce are the whole dish — simmer gently so the cakes don't crack, and let the sauce reduce until it clings like a glaze. If you can find real gochugaru, the flakes give the proper fiery red; if not, mild chilli flakes plus a little extra gochujang is honest. The anchovy-kelp stock is the street-vendor secret — worth the two extra minutes.","pairsWith":"Fried or plain gimbap (rice rolls) to mop the sauce · A boiled egg plopped in the sauce · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Tteokbokki started life in a palace kitchen as a royal dish — sliced rice cakes simmered in a soy-meat broth, the 'palace tteokbokki'. After the Korean War, a street vendor swapped the soy for the soldier-delivered gochujang that was suddenly everywhere, and the fiery red street version was born. The street version won: today the red tteokbokki is the dish, and it is one of Korea's best-selling street foods, served from the same vendor stalls in winter.","howThisFeels":"Glossy red cylinders of rice cake — the first bite is the purest sweet-spicy-chewy hit the Korean street has to offer, with the sauce sticking to your fingers and asking for more.","leftovers":["The classic Korean finish: keep the leftover red sauce and fry gimbap, or rice, in it — sauce-too-good-to-waste is part of the street-food code.","Add the leftover sauce to a pan of instant noodles for a cheat tteokbokki-ramen hybrid the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Street","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The original street bowl — chewy rice cakes in a glossy red sauce, with fish cake and a boiled egg as the classic companions.","trivia":"The street version includes paper-thin fish cakes by default — they flavour the sauce as they simmer and become the chew that balances the rice cake's chew. The boiled egg is the optional regular: halved into the sauce, it soaks up the red and becomes the best bite in the bowl.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes · 20g fish cakes (optional) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang · 8g gochugaru or mild chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":["Soak frozen rice cakes in cold water 10 minutes.","Simmer the stock (or water plus a scrap of kombu, removed after 5 minutes) in a pan.","Whisk in gochujang, gochugaru, soy and honey; bubble to a glossy red sauce.","Add the rice cakes and simmer gently 8–10 min until the sauce clings; add fish cake and egg for the last 3 min.","Spring onion off the heat; serve immediately."]},{"name":"Cheese Tteokbokki","icon":"🧀","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The modern favourite — molten mozzarella over the fiery red cakes, the fat calming the fire like a benediction.","trivia":"Cheese tteokbokki is the 21st-century Seoul standard — the volcanic-red sauce meets a cooling blanket of melted cheese and the combination became so popular it now rivals the original at street stalls.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g mozzarella, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Once the sauce has thickened and clings to the cakes, scatter the grated mozzarella over the top, cover the pan 1–2 minutes until it melts into a molten blanket, and eat with the cheese pulling off every cake."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-japchae","name":"Japchae","nameAlt":"The glass-noodle stir-fry that is a party's sweetest moment","aliases":["japchae","korean glass noodles","chap chae"],"course":"side","type":["side","noodles","banquet","vegetables","make-ahead"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","party","banquet","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles (dangmyeon – the round, slippery ones) · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot (fine matchsticks) · 30g onion (thinly sliced) · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms (soaked, sliced) · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":"THE NOODLES ARE SWEET-POTATO GLASS NOODLES AND THEY ARE THE WHOLE POINT. Japchae's signature is dangmyeon — translucent, glassy, gloriously slippery sweet-potato noodles. Cook them in boiling water 6–8 minutes until just tender, drain, and CUT them with scissors a few times so they are a manageable length — they are spectacularly springy. Do not rinse them; the starch coats them and helps the sauce cling.\n\n⚖️ EVERY COMPONENT IS COOKED SEPARATELY, THEN MIXED — THE HALLMARK OF A BANQUET DISH. This is not a one-pan stir-fry: the carrot, onion, mushrooms and spinach each take their own oil and their own minute, and each is seasoned at its turn. The separate cooking is what lets every colour and texture survive the final mix. HOLDING THE COMPONENTS APART IS THE WHOLE METHOD.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS A SWEET-SALTY GLOSS, MADE ALL AT ONCE. Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, minced garlic and a pinch of pepper — sweet forward, salty behind, the classic banchan balance. Have it in a cup before the pan goes on.\n\nSEAR THE BEEF FIRST IN ITS OWN MINUTE. Season it lightly, sear it hard and brief in a hot pan until just cooked — it should be pink to medium, never grey.\n\nNOW THE FINAL TOSS: ADD THE NOODLES, THE VEGETABLES, THE BEEF, THE SAUCE — TOSS ONCE, COVERED, AND LET EVERYTHING DRINK. Toss over low heat for 2 minutes so the sauce edges into everything, and finish with sesame seeds and a final thread of sesame oil. Serve warm or chilled — this is one of the great make-ahead dishes of Korean cooking and is better on day two.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 12g · Carbs 68g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Japchae is a legendary make-ahead — it keeps 3–4 days in the fridge and is genuinely better on day two as the sauce soaks in. Serve it cold or at room temperature, which is how it is traditionally offered at banquets. Freezes well for a month. Do not reheat hard — the noodles are perfect cool.","chefNotes":"Sweet potato glass noodles are non-negotiable. Cook every vegetable in its own pan with its own seasoning, keep them apart until the very end, then toss them all once in the sweet-salty sesame gloss. The separate cooking is what makes japchae japchae. Sesame oil, sesame seeds, soy and a spoon of sugar — the entire Korean binding spell.","pairsWith":"Korean barbecue (the classic banquet pairing) · Pickles · Cold noodles or soup on the side","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Japchae was born at the royal court in the early 1600s, when a court official served the king a dish of stir-fried vegetables and glass noodles so good it named itself: japchae means 'mixed vegetables'. It became a banquet standard and later went fully royal — King Gojong is said to have raised it to banquet status in the 19th century. Every celebration table in Korea still sets out a bowl.","howThisFeels":"Long, slippery, glassy noodles glistening with sesame oil and studded with bright vegetables — a banquet plate that somehow tastes better the next day.","leftovers":["The day-two move is the unsung one: serve it chilled, straight from the fridge — that is how Korean banquets offer it, and the cold noodles drink the sauce even deeper.","Fold leftover japchae into an omelette or a rice bowl with a fried egg for a five-minute Korean lunch."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beef","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"The banquet original — thin-sliced beef and bright vegetables through the glossy glass noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the royal-era version: thinly sliced beef, shiitake, carrot, spinach and onion, each cooked on its own and finished in the sweet sesame glaze. It is one of the most festive things you can put on a Korean table.","delta":{},"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":["Cook the glass noodles 6–8 min until tender; drain and cut with scissors.","Cook each vegetable separately in its own sesame-oiled pan, seasoning as you go.","Sear the beef hard and brief until pink, never grey.","Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, garlic and pepper.","Toss everything once with the sauce over low heat; finish with sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Mushroom (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"All the glassy-slick glory with mushrooms doing the beef's savoury work — the vegan banquet version.","trivia":"Simply removing the beef and browning an extra variety of mushrooms — shiitake, oyster or button — covers the savour beautifully, and a shot of soy in the mushroom pan deepens the whole plate. Japanese and Korean temple kitchens have been making an egg-free, beef-free japchae for centuries.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g extra mushrooms (oyster or button), sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the extra mushrooms hard in a dry hot pan until they give up their water and turn golden, then add a splash of soy and a drop of sesame to finish them. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. The mushrooms take the sweet-salty glaze beautifully and the plate loses nothing."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-samgyeopsal","name":"Samgyeopsal","nameAlt":"Grilled pork belly — the social dish Korean nights are built around","aliases":["samgyeopsal","grilled pork belly","korean bbq pork belly","ssam"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","grill","bbq","social","make-your-own"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","social","casual","groups"],"mood":"Social & festive","time":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","health":"Rich and indulgent — balance with raw greens and garlic, which are part of the ritual, not an afterthought.","allergen":"Soy (ssamjang, soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil in the ssamjang)","budget":"★☆☆ — belly is the treat; the lettuce-garlic-chilli table is cheap","audience":"Perfect for a weekend group — it is a do-it-yourself grill where nobody needs a plate, just lettuce and a lap.","season":"Best year-round; a summer braai where the grill IS the party and rain just moves it indoors","ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang (or mix 20g gochujang with 15g doenjang and a drop of honey) · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla (kkaennip, if you can find it) · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · freshly ground black pepper","method":"SAMGYEOPSAL IS NOT A RECIPE, IT IS A RITUAL — AND THE RITUAL IS THE TABLE. The meat arrives sizzling in the middle, and everyone cooks, wraps and eats at their own pace. The first rule is REFUSAL: samgyeopsal is NEVER marinated. The whole point is clean pork fat — the seasoning happens at the table in the wrap, which is why this is the one Korean meat that goes un-doctored onto the grill. The second rule is the grill itself. Lay the thick slices on a hot grill or heavy pan, cut-side down, and let them wallow in their own fat. Turn only when the underside is deeply golden and the edges are crisp — patient char beats constant fidgeting. As each piece cooks, cut it with scissors into bite-size strips right on the grill, letting the cut edges crisp another minute. This scissors-at-the-table move is not theatre; it keeps the juices inside and gives every tongue-sized piece a crunchy edge.\n\nWHILE IT COOKS, BUILD THE SSAM TABLE — this is half the dish. Down the centre goes a bowl of ssamjang (shop-bought is honestly fine; homemade is a 2-minute mix of gochujang, doenjang, a thread of sesame oil and a crush of garlic). Around it: cool lettuce leaves (or perilla if you can find it — its anise-and-mint edge with pork fat is one of the great Korean tastes), raw garlic sliced paper-thin, green chilli rings, and a little dish of seasoned sesame oil — sesame oil with a pinch of salt and black pepper, for a quick dip before the wrap.\n\nNOW THE WRAP THAT NAMES THE DISH: SSAM. Take a leaf, swipe a dab of ssamjang in the middle, lay in a piece of the hot pork with its crisp edge showing, a slice of raw garlic, a ring of chilli, fold the leaf over it like a little parcel and eat it whole in one mouthful. Do not nibble — a ssam is built for a single satisfying bite, and the cold leaf against the hot fat, the raw garlic, the chilli and the salty paste colliding at once is exactly why Koreans eat this nightly.\n\nA bowl of steamed rice is optional but honest: for the last few pieces, grill the cloves of leftover garlic alongside the pork until they are soft and sweet, and tuck them into the final wrap. Wash down with cold soju or beer, and repeat until the grill is empty.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","kcal":"~550 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 6g · Fat 46g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best eaten at the grill, fresh from the pan. Cooked leftover belly keeps 2 days in the fridge — the fat sets and it is superb cold in rice or sliced into fried rice. Do not reheat hard; the belly turns from crisp to wobbly.","chefNotes":"Never marinate it — samgyeopsal is pure pork, and the table is the seasoning. Char it patiently, cut it at the table, and never skimp the garlic and lettuce: they are not garnishes, they are the balance that makes a fat-soaked meal feel light.","pairsWith":"Cold soju or crisp lager · Kimchi · Doenjang-jjigae on the side · More lettuce — always more lettuce","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Doenjang-jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Samgyeopsal means 'three-layered flesh' — and the name is about the cut, not the cooking. Pork belly is one of the most popular foods in Korea precisely because it is unmarinated: while almost every other meat arrives drenched in soy or gochujang, this one is served raw at the table, and the seasoning happens in the lettuce wrap. Korean barbecue restaurants built entirely around samgyeopsal number in the thousands, and the 'samgyeopsal + soju' combo is so iconic it has its own slang name: sam-sam, from the similar syllables. The ritual of one person cooking for the table, cutting the meat with scissors and tucking it into leaves for friends first, is so deeply social that Koreans debate whether it tastes better shared — and the honest answer is yes.","howThisFeels":"The sizzle of unmarinated belly curling at the edges, the bright sting of raw garlic and chilli against cool lettuce, and the whole table reaching for the ssamjang at once — nobody talks much during samgyeopsal, and everyone is happy.","leftovers":["Chop leftover belly small and fry it with kimchi, cold rice and a spoon of gochujang for samgyeopsal kimchi fried rice — the classic next-day move.","Slice it thin and crown a bowl of steaming rice with a rare soft egg for one of Korea's favourite solo comfort lunches.","Use the leftover ssamjang as a rapid stir-fry sauce — a tablespoon with any chop of vegetables becomes an instant Korean bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Grill (Samgyeopsal)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":60,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — thick belly, patient char, scissors at the table, and the ssam table dressed with raw garlic and chilli.","trivia":"The classic is the complete experience: the unmarinated belly, the ssamjang-and-garlic table, and the lettuce-wrap rule of one full bite per ssam.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · black pepper","method":["Grill the unmarinated belly slices cut-side down, turning only when deeply golden; cut into strips with scissors at the table.","Set the ssam table: ssamjang, lettuce or perilla, raw garlic, chilli rings, and seasoned sesame oil.","Press each piece into the salt, swipe with ssamjang, and wrap in a leaf with garlic and chilli — one whole bite per wrap.","Repeat until the grill is empty, washing down with cold soju or beer."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Pan-Fried Belly Strips","icon":"💰","costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The same unmarinated pork, but sliced thin and pan-fried fast instead of grilled — the apartment weeknight version that still gets the ssam table.","trivia":"Slicing the belly thin (about 5mm) is the budget-quick trick: it cooks in two minutes a side in a hot pan with no grill required, and because it is thinner it needs less total fat rendered. The ssam table does the rest of the work exactly as the classic.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g pork belly, in thick slices","to":"200g pork belly, pre-sliced thin (about 5mm)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A thin slice needs only a minute or two per side in a hot, dry pan. Let the first side go a full 90 seconds untouched so it develops a proper golden crust, then flip, stack the slices into a little pile, and serve immediately with the ssam table. No scissors needed — they are already bite-sized."}]}},{"name":"Grilled Tofu & Mushroom Ssam (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same ssam ritual, with the grill given over to tofu and mushrooms — and honestly, this could be the best version: charred tofu soaks up the ssamjang and the fun of the wrap is unchanged.","trivia":"Korean temple kitchens have always eaten this way — grilling firm tofu until it holds a golden crust and wrapping it in lettuce with ssamjang. The tofu does what the belly cannot: it drinks the paste rather than shedding fat, and the sesame-oil seasoning that replaces pork fat brings a nutty depth of its own.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g pork belly, in thick slices"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g firm tofu, cut into thick slabs"},{"item":"100g shiitake or other mushrooms, thick-sliced"},{"item":"extra sesame oil for brushing"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu slabs dry, brush both faces with sesame oil, and grill until deeply golden on both sides — a proper crust, not a pale surface. Brush the mushrooms with the same oil and grill until they release their water and take colour. Build the wraps exactly as the classic: ssamjang, garlic, chilli, leaf. For a fully vegan bowl, make ssamjang from gochujang and doenjang and a drop of maple instead of honey."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-sundubu-jjigae","name":"Sundubu-jjigae","nameAlt":"Soft tofu stew — the bubbling volcano pot Koreans eat on cold nights","aliases":["sundubu","soft tofu stew","sundubu jjigae","tofu stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","tofu","spicy","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","lunch"],"mood":"Comforting & restorative","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","health":"High-protein and light — soft tofu is genuinely good for you, and this is one of the lightest comfort stews in Korean cooking.","allergen":"Soy (tofu, soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang in some versions) · seafood in the classic (optional — remove for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — tofu and pantry staples; a little pork or seafood stretches the classic, or skip them entirely for the cheapest honest bowl","audience":"Perfect for anyone who needs a gentle, warming bowl — it is Korea's fever-and-cold-night food for good reason.","season":"Winter and rainy days are its home, but Koreans crave it year-round","ingredients":"1 packet soft/silken tofu (about 300g) · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock (or vegetable stock for vegan) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or 1 tbsp mild chilli flakes + a pinch more heat · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1 egg (optional — classic) · 40g pork belly or 50g prawns (optional — classic) · black pepper","method":"THE STEW LIVES IN A STONE POT, AND THE POT IS HALF THE DISH. The classic is cooked in a ttukbaegi — a small earthenware pot that sits on the stove bubbling, and arrives still bubbling at the table, the tofu quivering like it is alive. No ttukbaegi? A small heavy saucepan does the same job; the drama is just quieter.\n\nFIRST, THE STOCK — THIS IS WHERE THE DEPTH COMES FROM. True sundubu is built on anchovy-kelp stock (see the Dashi card for the technique family), and it is what separates this from a box of tofu in chilli water. Simmer the big kelp scrap in the stock base with dried anchovies for 5 minutes, then remove them. For a vegan pot, use vegetable stock with a strip of dried shiitake soaked in for the same savoury depth.\n\n⚖️ THE SPICE LAYER IS BUILT IN THE PAN, NOT DUMPED IN. Heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the garlic and onion, and fry gently for a minute; then the gochugaru and let it bloom in the oil for 30 raw seconds — this is the step that turns flat chilli powder into a rich, red, fragrant base. Stir in the gochujang and soy until the paste dissolves into the oil.\n\nNOW THE STOCK AND THE TOFU. Pour in the stock and bring to a bubble. Add the protein if using — pork belly slices or prawns — and simmer 5 minutes. Then the starring act: spoon the soft tofu in big, impossibly fragile pieces, letting them float rather than stirring them into nothing. The tofu is never cut with a knife and never chopped in a bowl; it is scooped in whole and breaks apart naturally as the stew bubbles — that irregular, patchy texture is the soul of sundubu.\n\n⚖️ THE EGG GOES IN AT THE TABLE, NOT THE STOVE. Crack it into the bubbling centre, turn the heat off, and let the residual bubbling cook the white while the yolk stays molten. The yolk is the finishing sauce of the stew, broken with a spoon right before eating. Serve immediately, straight from the pot, with a bowl of steamed rice — the Korean rule is to alternate bite of stew, bite off plain rice, because the rice is the palette that makes the heat sing, not the thing to be drowned in it.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 24g · Carbs 18g · Fat 24g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Sundubu is at its best the moment it is made — the soft tofu is at its silkiest straight from the bubbling pot. Leftover stew keeps a day in the fridge, but the tofu weeps and firms; reheat gently and add a fresh spoon of stock to re-liquefy. The egg is always fresh, cracked at the table.","chefNotes":"Three rules make or break sundubu: a proper kelp-anchovy stock (the depth is in the broth, not the chilli), the spice bloomed in oil before the liquid goes in, and the tofu scooped in whole and never stirred. The egg is the finishing act, always at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of plain steamed rice · Kimchi · The same leaves of seasoned seaweed · Cold barley tea between spoonfuls","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Korean Fried Chicken","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Sundubu-jjigae means 'soft tofu stew', and the word 'also' hides real history: sundubu is a Korean-style tofu that is older and firmer than Japanese silken tofu, made the traditional way with natural seawater as the coagulant. The stew became iconic in the 1980s when Korean immigrant grandmothers opened 'sundubu' restaurants in Los Angeles, and the bubbling stone-pot bowl quickly became a Korean-American institution — today it is one of the most-ordered items in Korean food, famous for arriving at the table still boiling, with the raw egg in the diner's control.","howThisFeels":"The whole pot arriving at the table still bubbling, the soft tofu wobbling like a live thing in red broth, and that first warm spoonful of stew followed by a cooling bite of plain rice — pure comfort in liquid form.","leftovers":["Spoon the leftover stew over cold rice with a sesame-oil thread for a warm-cold jjigae donburi — the absorbed broth is the point.","Freeze the stew base (before the tofu and egg) in a tub; a fresh scoop of soft tofu and 5 minutes rebuilds the whole pot.","Drain the stew solids and stir them into an omelette with a little extra sesame — the captured umami disappears into the eggs."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork & Soft Tofu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The classic balance — a few slices of pork belly give the broth a gentle richness that the soft tofu then cuts right through.","trivia":"Pork and tofu are a classic Korean pairing — the meat adds a little fat and a little sweetness to the otherwise clean, fiery broth, and the soft tofu swallows it all. This is the everyday home version, not the seafood restaurant one.","delta":{},"ingredients":"1 packet soft tofu · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 30g onion · 1 spring onion · 1 egg · 40g pork belly, sliced thin · black pepper","method":["Simmer kelp and anchovies in the stock base 5 minutes; strain.","Bloom the garlic and gochugaru in sesame oil, then the gochujang and soy.","Add the stock, then the pork belly; simmer 5 minutes.","Spoon in the tofu in big fragile pieces — never stir it.","Bubble 3 minutes, crack the egg in at the table, serve with rice."]},{"name":"Budget — Egg & Kimchi Only (Vegan-friendly)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"No pork, no seafood — just good stock, bloomed chilli and the star of the show, soft tofu. The cheapest honest bowl and genuinely the nearest to the soul of the dish.","trivia":"The temple version of sundubu is exactly this — no meat, no fish broth, just depth from kelp and shiitake, and this is how many Koreans actually eat it at home. The egg is optional; skip it for a fully vegan pot.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g pork belly, sliced thin"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g kimchi, chopped"},{"item":"extra shiitake for the stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the chopped kimchi with the stock for its fermented tang and a deeper colour. Use a vegetable stock properly deepened with dried shiitake and kelp — the umami carries the whole bowl with no meat at all. Skip the egg for the fully vegan version, or keep it for the classic yolk-as-sauce finish."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Sundubu","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Bottled anchovy stock and a supermarket packet of soft tofu — the quickest honest sundubu, still bubbling, still with the egg at the table.","trivia":"Even in Korea, the weekday shortcut is a good bottled stock and a packet of soft tofu from the convenience store. The depth that matters is the spice bloom and the never-stir tofu rule — those are the techniques, not the homemade stock.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a quality bottled anchovy-kelp stock (or instant dashi stock powder, the legitimate Korean shortcut) in place of the homemade simmer. Everything else identical — the bloom, the tofu spooned whole, the egg at the table. The whole pot comes together in about 15 minutes."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-fried-chicken","name":"Yangnyeom Korean Fried Chicken","nameAlt":"Double-fried chicken in a glossy gochujang glaze — chimaek's other half","aliases":["yangnyeom chicken","korean fried chicken","kfc korean","double fried chicken","chimaek"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","party","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","comfort","sports","everyday"],"mood":"High-energy & celebratory","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min (including the double fry)","health":"Fried — this is an indulgence, but the double fry actually renders MORE fat out of the skin, leaving it crisper AND slightly leaner than a single fry.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · gluten (flour in the coating)","budget":"★★☆ — chicken pieces are cheap; the sauce is pantry staples","audience":"The party platter — made for sharing with cold beer (chimaek) and fingers.","season":"Game night and celebration food, year-round — but the craving strikes hardest with cold beer in hand","ingredients":"300g chicken pieces (wings or thighs) · 40g cornflour + 20g plain flour for the coating · 1 egg (optional, for a thicker crust) · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru or 1 tbsp sweet chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce (the Korean fusion nod) · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · toasted sesame seeds · oil for frying","method":"THE DOUBLE FRY IS NOT A TECHNIQUE, IT IS THE RECIPE — and it is the entire reason Korean fried chicken eats like a different animal from any other fried chicken. The first fry at a lower heat cooks the chicken through and pushes moisture and fat out; a 5-minute rest; then a SECOND fry at high heat in the same oil, which turns the already-set crust glassy and shatter-crisp. That second crunch is what survives the glaze — a single-fried chicken goes soggy the instant the sauce touches it; a double-fried one stays crackly for an hour. The cornflour in the coating is the second secret: cornflour has no gluten, so it fries into a light, crisp shell instead of a chewy wheat one.\n\nBEFORE YOU FRY, THE COATING. Season the chicken and toss it in a dry mix of 2 parts cornflour to 1 part plain flour. The dry-coat matters: a dry, powdery coat is what blisters into that airy crackle. Rest the coated pieces 10 minutes so the coating sets and grips.\n\nTHE FIRST FRY: 160–165°C, about 7 minutes, until pale gold and just cooked through. The oil should be at a steady gentle bubble — too hot and the outside scorches before the middle cooks. Lift out onto a rack (never paper towel, which steams the bottom), and rest 5 minutes.\n\nTHE SECOND FRY: crank the oil to 185–190°C and fry again 3–4 minutes until deep golden and rock-hard crisp. Drain on the rack again.\n\n⚖️ THE GLAZE IS A PAN, NOT A BOIL. In a separate pan over medium heat, soften the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame oil and garlic together for a minute until glossy and warm — the gochujang is the soul: a fermented chilli paste whose slow umami and gentle heat is why this tastes deep, not just spicy-sweet. Toss the hot chicken through the warm glaze just enough to coat, then out. (For the absolute crispiest version, brush the glaze on instead of tossing — this is the wing-specialist's move.)\n\nFINISH with sesame seeds and sliced spring onion, and eat immediately with cold beer — the pairing is so sacred in Korea it has its own name: chimaek (chi from chicken, maek from beer).","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~600 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 48g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best straight from the fryer while the crunch sings. Keeps 2 days in the fridge — but re-crisp in a hot oven for 5 minutes, never the microwave, which ruins the glassy crust on contact. The glaze, refrigerated separately, keeps a week and vanishes over noodles.","chefNotes":"Double-fry or don't bother — the second fry is the entire dish. Dry-coat with cornflour for a light glassy shell. Glaze from a pan, not a boil. And brush the glaze if you want maximum crunch; toss it if you want maximum clove-on-garlic coating.","pairsWith":"Cold beer (non-negotiable — it IS chimaek) · Pickled radish (the classic yellow cubes, or a sharp local pickle) · More cold beer","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Korean fried chicken's rise is one of the great fried-food stories. American soldiers brought Southern fried chicken to Korea after the Korean War, and Korean cooks immediately saw the improvement: add a double fry and a sticky-sweet-spicy glaze, and the dish became its own creature — Korea's most-loved delivery food, with fried-chicken restaurants numbering in the tens of thousands. The drink pairing (chimaek) is so culturally wired that marriage-hungry singles in Korea are said to have tried 'chimaek blind dates' — first dates built entirely on chicken and beer. And the glaze's secret ingredient is often a whisper of tomato sauce: not a Western intrusion but a Korean fusion that has been standard since the 1980s, balancing the gochujang's savour with a touch of sweetness.","howThisFeels":"The impossible crunch of the second fry, then the glossy red glaze hitting hot chicken — the smell of garlic and gochujang filling the kitchen, and someone already twisting the cap off a cold beer.","leftovers":["Chop cold leftover pieces small and toss them through rice with the glaze and sesame for a 5-minute yangnyeom fried-rice bowl.","Cold chicken, pulled off the bone, is the star of a kimchi fried-rice with a fried egg the next morning.","The leftover glaze is a sauce for anything — noodles, tofu, or just a spoon (nobody is judging at home)."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Yangnyeom (Gochujang Glaze)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The full double-fry with the glossy sweet-spicy-gochujang glaze — the chimaek centrepiece.","trivia":"The classic is the 1980s Seoul formulation — double-fried wings in a gochujang-honey-garlic-tomato glaze. It is the dish that made Korean fried chicken famous worldwide.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g chicken pieces · 40g cornflour · 20g plain flour · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce · 2 cloves garlic · spring onion · sesame seeds · oil","method":["Dry-coat seasoned chicken in cornflour-flour mix; rest 10 minutes.","First fry at 160°C, 7 minutes — pale gold, just cooked; rest on a rack 5 minutes.","Second fry at 190°C, 3-4 minutes — deep gold and rock-crisp.","Glaze in a warm pan: gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame, garlic.","Toss hot chicken through the warm glaze; finish with sesame and spring onion. Serve with cold beer — it is chimaek."]},{"name":"Budget — Single-Fry Soy-Garlic Sticky","icon":"💰","costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"No double fry, a simpler soy-garlic-sugar glaze — still properly crisped and sticky, just without the glass-shatter of the full method.","trivia":"The budget fork trades the second fry for a longer single fry at a steady medium heat, which gets most of the crunch — and swaps the gochujang glaze for a classic soy-garlic-sugar sticky sauce, which is arguably the more traditional Korean flavour (the gochujang glaze is the newer star).","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the coated chicken in a single batch at a steady 175°C for 10-12 minutes until deep golden and fully cooked, then drain on a rack. For the glaze, simmer soy, brown sugar and crushed garlic to a shine — a spoon of gochujang optional. Toss and serve. Most of the crunch, all of the flavour, about half the oil temperature juggling."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Crispy Oven Wings with Yangnyeom","icon":"⚡","costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The weeknight recipe — dry-baked wings got properly crisp in a hot oven, then the same glossy gochujang glaze.","trivia":"A sprinkle of baking powder over the dry-coated wings, baked at a high heat, crisps them surprisingly close to a fry — the baking powder breaks down into tiny bubbles on the skin. It is the classic Korean-American home shortcut.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Toss the coated wings with ½ tsp baking powder, spread on a rack over a tray, and bake at 220°C for 40 minutes, turning once, until deeply golden and crisp. Warm the glaze, coat, and serve — the oven does the double-fry's job from the outside in."}]}},{"name":"Sticky Crispy Tofu (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The impossible-crunch tofu version — the same glossy gochujang glaze, and tofu that cracks like it belongs in the double-fry tray.","trivia":"Firm tofu pressed, coated in cornflour and double-fried gives a glassy shell that genuinely holds the glaze — the Korean vegan-fried-chicken answer, and it is better than it has any right to be.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"300g chicken pieces"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g firm tofu, pressed and cut into thick slabs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu 20 minutes, cut into slabs, coat in cornflour and double-fry exactly as the classic. The cornflour crust is what gives the crackle. For a fully vegan glaze, swap the honey for brown sugar — everything else is naturally vegan (gochujang, soy, tomato sauce)."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-jjigae","name":"Kimchi Jjigae","nameAlt":"Kimchi stew — the fridge song that uses up OLD kimchi","aliases":["kimchi jjigae","kimchi stew","doenjang kimchi stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","kimchi","one-pot","comfort","lunch"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","winter","comfort","leftover"],"mood":"Warm, tangy & soulful","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","health":"Fermented and probiotic — the older kimchi used here is richest in lactobacilli, and this is one of Korea's most comforting fermented meals.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang, tofu) · seafood in the classic tuna version (swap for 'tofu-only' for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — one of the cheapest comfort stews there is, built on the kimchi already in the fridge","audience":"The everyday home stew — a bowl of kimchi jjigae is Korea's answer to a grilled-cheese-and-soup night.","season":"Winter is its throne — the colder the day, the better the jjigae — but it rescues nothing-but-old-kimchi nights year-round","ingredients":"100g OLD kimchi (the older, the deeper — days to weeks old is ideal) · 1 knob pork belly or a small tin of tuna (optional, the richness) · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · a pinch of sugar · black pepper","method":"THE STEW IS A FRIDGE-CLEARING SONG, WRITTEN AROUND OLD KIMCHI — and the older the kimchi, the better the jjigae. Fresh kimchi snaps and fizzes like a jar salad; ageing kimchi turns deeply sour, effervescent and mellow, and it is that aged sourness that carries a whole stew. If your kimchi is young and bright, the honest fix is a splash of vinegar and a pinch of sugar to fake the fermentation curve, and let it cook longer.\n\n⚖️ THE KIMCHI IS SEARED BEFORE IT MEETS ANY LIQUID. This is the step nobody skips in a proper Korean kitchen: heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the drained, roughly chopped kimchi, and fry it HARD until its edges char and the whole pan smells intensely of fermented chilli — about 3 minutes. Raw kimchi dropped into broth tastes like soup with kimchi in it; seared kimchi flavours the entire stew from within. Save the kimchi juice too — a spoon of it goes in with the liquid for extra tang.\n\nNOW THE PROTEIN. Press the pork belly slices (or drained tuna) into the seared kimchi and let them brown with it — this is the 1-minute step that loans the whole pot its deep, savoury backbone. If you are keeping it vegan, skip straight to the broth.\n\nIN WITH THE LIQUID AND THE SAVOURY PASTE. Add the water (or stock), the gochujang, soy, garlic and that pinch of sugar (the sugar bridges the ferment's sourness and the gochujang's funk). Simmer 15 minutes until the kimchi is soft and the broth is red and glossy. The stew can be thin — jjigae is brothier than a curry — but it should coat the spoon.\n\n⚖️ THE TOFU GOES IN LAST, AND STAYS A QUIET FOOTNOTE. Cube firm tofu — never soft, in this stew — add it in the last 5 minutes, and let the heat soak it without stirring it to breaking. Serve bubbling hot with a bowl of steamed rice beside, and alternate bites: stew, then plain rice, then stew. The plain rice is not a side in Korea; it is the cooling palate that lets the heat keep singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 18g · Carbs 16g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"Kimchi jjigae is famously better on day two — the kimchi keeps fermenting and the flavour rounds. Keeps 4 days in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water. Freezes well in portions for a month. The tofu is best added fresh each time.","chefNotes":"The three rules: USE OLD KIMCHI (the sourness is the heart), SEAR THE KIMCHI hard before the liquid goes in (this is where the flavour builds), and ADD THE TOFU LAST so it stays intact. A spoon of kimchi juice in the broth is the classic extra tang.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · More kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang-jjigae or a quick side of cucumber banchan","goesWith":["Sundubu Jjigae","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi jjigae is the true kitchen-sink stew of Korea — the opposite of the fresh, careful banchan. It exists entirely as a use for OLD kimchi: the jars that have been fermenting in the fridge for days and weeks, too sour and mellow to eat raw as banchan, are exactly right for this pot. Every Korean household has a version, and the family argument is usually about pork vs tuna — the pork version is richer, and a small tin of tuna (a beloved budget addition) brings a mellow fishiness that pairs shockingly well with the sour kimchi. In fact, the tuna version is so common in Korean kitchens that tinned tuna and kimchi are considered a match made in the pantry. In Korea the stew is often the winter comfort food that marks the return of cold weather — the first kimchi-jjigae night of autumn is practically a seasonal ritual.","howThisFeels":"The smell of kimchi searing hard in sesame oil before the liquid even goes in — then a bubbling red pot on the table, the tofu bobbing, and the first spoonful that is somehow both sour and comforting all the way down.","leftovers":["The day-two jjigae is a matter of pride — reheat gently, add a splash of water, and the fermented broth is even deeper.","Spoon it over cold rice and top with an egg for a lazy jjigae donburi.","Stir a spoonful into fried rice or noodles for an instant flavour bomb."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Belly Kimchi Jjigae","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seared kimchi, browned pork slices, and a long simmer that turns the broth red, glossy and deeply savoury.","trivia":"Pork and kimchi are one of Korea's most classic pairings — the pork's fat rounds out the fierce sourness of old kimchi, and the searing step melds them into one flavour before the broth even enters.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi · 1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 1 spring onion · pinch of sugar · pepper","method":["Sear the drained old kimchi hard in hot sesame oil, 3 minutes, until its edges char.","Press the pork slices in and brown with it, 1 minute.","Add the stock, gochujang, soy, garlic and sugar; simmer 15 minutes until red and glossy.","Add the firm tofu in cubes for the last 5 minutes, never stirring it apart.","Serve bubbling hot with steamed rice — alternate stew and plain rice in every bite."]},{"name":"Budget — Tuna Kimchi Jjigae (Bonus: Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"A small tin of tuna replaces the pork — the beloved budget version that turns out to be the pantry match of the century. Skip the tuna and keep the kelp stock for a fully vegan pot.","trivia":"Tinned tuna in kimchi jjigae is not a poverty substitute — it is a beloved Korean classic in its own right. The drained tuna gets browned with the seared kimchi like the pork would, and its mellow fishiness against the sour fermented chilli is one of those pairings that makes no sense until you taste it.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced","to":"1 small tin of tuna, drained (or skip entirely for vegan)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna and press it into the seared kimchi in place of the pork, letting it brown with the pan — the flakes soak up the fermented oil and carry it through the stew. For the vegan version, skip the tuna and use a deep kelp-and-shiitake stock; gochujang carries the body."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Kimchi Stew","icon":"⚡","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Bottled stock and pre-cubed tofu turn the classic into a genuine 15-minute throw-together — the recipe exists for the night the fridge offers nothing but old kimchi, tofu and a busy day.","trivia":"Even fast, the two rules that matter survive: the kimchi gets seared hard before the liquid goes in, and the tofu is added at the end. The stew is as good as the kimchi, and the kimchi is the whole recipe in miniature.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a strong bottled anchovy or vegetable stock, cube the tofu, and skip any browning beyond the kimchi's essential sear. With the kimchi seared and everything else poured together, the pot is on the table in a quarter-hour. The garlic and a pinch of sugar are still non-negotiable."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-gimbap","name":"Gimbap","nameAlt":"Seaweed rice rolls — the Korean picnic and road-trip food","aliases":["gimbap","kimbap","korean sushi roll","seaweed rice roll"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","rolls","picnic","lunchbox","no-cook"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["picnic","lunchbox","road-trip","lunch","everyday"],"mood":"Fresh, cheerful & portable","time":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min (no cooking of the filling beyond what you prep)","health":"Remarkably balanced — rice, lean protein, bright vegetables and sesame oil, with far less sugar or sauce than most lunch options.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil in the rice and filling) · soy (soy sauce in some fillings) · gluten (optional ham/nori)","budget":"★★★ — rice, a few vegetables, and one sheet of nori; it is Korea's classic cheap portable lunch","audience":"The lunchbox and picnic hero — children love it, adults find it satisfying, and it travels better than any sandwich.","season":"Year-round; spring picnics and summer holidays are its natural home","ingredients":"150g cooked short-grain rice (freshly steamed, warm) · 1 sheet nori (seaweed for sushi rolls) · 1 egg, beaten · 40g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks and stir-fried briefly · 40g spinach, wilted and seasoned · 30g pickled radish (danmuji, or a sharp pickle) · 1 slice ham OR 40g bulgogi beef (optional) · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · a few drops of rice vinegar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"GIMBAP IS KOREA'S ROAD-TRIP FOOD — the rolled seaweed-rice snack that travels in the boot (or picnic basket) and gets eaten by hand beside the highway. It is not sushi; it is Korean through and through, seasoned with sesame oil rather than sushi vinegar's sharpness. The two rules that make it: WARM, SEASONED RICE and a ROLL CLOSED TIGHT.\n\n⚖️ THE RICE IS THE WHOLE TRICK — WARM, NOT HOT, AND SEASONED, NOT PLAIN. Cook the rice, and while it is still warm (too hot steams the nori soggy; too cold and the roll falls apart), season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt — and just a few drops of rice vinegar, the lightest echo of near-sushi. Freshly steamed rice is essential: day-old rice is dry and won't stick together in a roll.\n\n⚖️ EVERY FILLING IS PREPPED SEPARATELY, KOREAN-BANCHAN-STYLE. Cut the carrot into fine matchsticks and stir-fry them in a little sesame oil with a pinch of salt until just tender; wilt the spinach, squeeze it DRY (wet filling is the top gimbap killer) and season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt. The pickled radish (danmuji) — if you can find it, it is the bright-yellow punch that defines gimbap — is used raw. The egg: make a thin omelette sheet and slice it into ribbons.\n\nNOW THE ROLL. Lay the nori shiny-side down on a bamboo mat (or, honestly, a clean tea towel works). Spread the warm seasoned rice in an EVEN, THIN layer across the whole sheet, leaving a bare 2cm strip along the far edge — that bare edge is the glue, and a thin rice layer is the difference between a delicate roll and a bready one. Line the fillings in a row just below the centre: the colourful stripes of carrot, spinach, egg, radish and the ham or beef.\n\n⚖️ ROLL IT TIGHT, LIKE A CIGAR, AND SEAL WITH WATER. The signature move of good gimbap is the tight, even roll — pull the mat's near edge up and over, press the filling into a cylinder, and roll forward firmly, compressing as you go. Dip your finger in water, run it along the bare nori edge, and seal the seam. Rest the sealed roll seam-down for a minute.\n\nSLICE with a very sharp, slightly wet knife — sawing drags the rice out. Brush the finished roll lightly with sesame oil and scatter toasted sesame seeds over it if you want the classic glistening finish. Slice into bite-size rounds and pack them for the journey.\n\nREGIONAL NOTE FOR THE GLOBAL READER: danmuji (the yellow pickled radish) is gimbap's secret weapon and almost defines the taste — it is a bright, sweet-tangy pickle sold at Asian grocers. A sharp pickled cucumber or a good chow-chow-style pickle stands in honestly if you cannot find it.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 68g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Gimbap is made for the road but is best eaten the same day — the nori softens and the rice dries with time. Keeps a day wrapped tightly in plastic, or up to 2 days in the fridge if the roll is very tight. To revive, brush with sesame oil and wrap in a damp cloth for an hour — the classic picnic trick.","chefNotes":"The two make-or-break rules: warm, sesame-oiled rice (never dry day-old) and a tight roll. Wet filling is the silent killer — squeeze every filling dry. The bare nori edge sealed with water, and slicing with a wet knife, are the two professional moves that turn a roll into gimbap.","pairsWith":"A can of cold cola or barley tea · Kimchi on the side · A crisp soup (many Koreans eat gimbap with a side of mandu-guk or a simple egg drop soup)","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Gimbap evolved from Japanese nori-maki during the Japanese occupation, but it became entirely its own: Korean gimbap uses sesame-oil-seasoned rice (not vinegar-heavy sushi rice), a wider range of cooked vegetables, and is eaten as everyday portable food rather than ceremony. The word gimbap means simply 'seaweed-rice', gim being the Korean word for the nori sheet. It is so tied to travel that Koreans call it 'the official food of the Korean road trip' — sold in bulk at rest stops and gas-station convenience stores, picked up on the way to both the beach and the mountains. The yellow danmuji pickle inside is so essential that any Korean grocery sells tubs of it specifically for gimbap making, and the number of gimbap franchises in Korea runs into the thousands.","howThisFeels":"Unrolling the mat to reveal a perfect glossy cylinder, slicing it into neat rounds with a wet knife — and the small triumph of a bite that is rice, egg, sesame and pickle all at once, eaten warm by the side of the road or cool from the lunchbox.","leftovers":["Fried gimbap (savoury leftover move): slice the day-old rolls thick and pan-fry them in a little oil until golden — the Korean way to make yesterday's gimbap exciting again.","Wrap leftover gimbap in a damp cloth for an hour to revive the nori before eating.","Dice it into a rice bowl with a fried egg for gimbap-style fried rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bulgogi Gimbap","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seasoned rice, carrot, spinach, egg ribbon, pickled radish and a strip of the marinated beef (or ham). The full gimbap experience.","trivia":"Bulgogi gimbap is the upmarket home version — strips of the pear-marinated beef add a savoury-sweet depth that plain ham cannot match. In Korea the convenience-store version is the daily standard; the home version with good beef is for guests.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g warm short-grain rice · 1 sheet nori · 1 egg · 40g carrot · 40g spinach · 30g pickled radish (danmuji) · 1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · rice vinegar · sesame seeds","method":["Season the warm rice with sesame oil, salt and a few drops of vinegar.","Prep the fillings separately: stir-fried carrot, wilted-and-squeezed spinach, egg ribbon (thin omelette sheet sliced), pickled radish.","Spread rice in a thin even layer on the nori, leaving a 2cm bare edge.","Line the colourful fillings just below centre, with the beef or ham as the anchor.","Roll TIGHT with a mat, seal the bare edge with water, rest seam-down.","Slice with a wet knife; brush with sesame oil and scatter sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Tuna Mayo Gimbap","icon":"💰","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Tinned tuna mixed with a little mayo stands in for the pricier beef — the beloved budget filling that the whole country orders from convenience stores.","trivia":"Tuna-mayo gimbap is a genuine Korean favourite, not a compromise one: the creamy tuna against the sesame rice and crunchy pickle is why it is one of the best-selling convenience-store gimbaps in Korea.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef","to":"80g tinned tuna, drained, mixed with a little mayo and a pinch of sesame"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna well, mix with a spoonful of mayo, a few drops of sesame oil and a pinch of salt. Use it exactly where the beef would sit. It rolls tighter with a softer filling, so press gently as you roll."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian (Yachae) Gimbap","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"Every colour the roll can carry — carrot, spinach, egg-free, pickled radish, cucumber — and honestly the crispest, freshest version of all.","trivia":"Vegetable gimbap (yachae gimbap) is a standard make in Korean households, and the pickled radish is what carries its bright tang. For a fully vegan roll, simply skip the egg and use seasoned tofu or just more vegetables — the roll loses nothing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g extra vegetables (cucumber, seasoned tofu) in place of the protein and egg"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add cucumber matchsticks and a strip of seasoned or smoked tofu for the savoury anchor. Squeeze the cucumber dry so the roll stays crisp. The sesame-oiled rice and the pickle do the rest — this is the freshest, most colourful gimbap of the set."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-haemul-pajeon","name":"Haemul Pajeon","nameAlt":"Seafood & spring onion pancake — Korea's rain-day comfort","aliases":["haemul pajeon","seafood pancake","korean pancake","pajeon"],"course":"main","type":["main","pancake","seafood","street-food","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","street-food","comfort","autumn"],"mood":"Comforting & casual","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 15 min","health":"Moderately rich (pan-fried) but genuinely vegetable-forward — the spring onions are the base, not a garnish.","allergen":"Seafood (prawn, squid — skip for vegan) · gluten (wheat flour) · egg (optional batter) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — flour and spring onions are cheap; a little seafood stretches a big sharing pancake","audience":"The sharing starter or rain-day supper — cooked whole at the table for a crowd, or quartered as a snack.","season":"A Korean rain-day tradition — the sizzle is the sound of rain, and the pancake is made to be shared","ingredients":"100g spring onions (about 5-6, whole stems) · 50g prawns, chopped · 50g squid or calamari rings, chopped · 80g cake flour · 20g cornflour · 1 egg (optional, for a crisper batter) · 180ml cold water (or light stock) · 5ml soy sauce · 10ml dipping sauce: soy + a splash of vinegar + a pinch of chilli flakes + a drop of sesame oil · pinch of salt · black pepper · oil for frying","method":"PAJEON IS THE PAN-CAKE OF RAIN DAYS — Koreans reach for it the moment the first drops fall, because the sizzle of batter in the pan sounds exactly like rain, and it is a dish built for sharing straight from the pan. THE PRINCIPLE: the spring onions are not a topping, they are the STRUCTURE. You want long, whole-to-halved green stems laid flat like a raft in the pan, not chopped through the batter. Noodle-like strands of onion in a thin, crispy pancake is the look and the mouthfeel of a proper pajeon.\n\nTHE BATTER IS THIN, AND THE SECRET IS COLD WATER. Whisk the flour, cornflour, a pinch of salt, pepper and the cold water (or light stock) into a loose, lump-free batter about the thickness of double cream — noticeably thinner than a Western pancake. Cold liquid is the trick: a cold batter lets the outside crisp while the inside steams, giving that crispy-outside, soft-inside classic pajeon texture. Add the optional egg for a slightly richer, more tender crust.\n\nTHE PAN IS THE SECOND SECRET — FILM OF OIL, THEN THE SHOW. Heat a wide, non-stick pan and coat it with a good slick of oil. Lay the spring onion stems across the pan in a tight log — a thick bundle, thicker than feels right. Pour the batter over the top so it seeps through and around the onions and spreads to the pan's edge: the pancake should look like a golden disc with green onion veins showing through.\n\nDOT THE SEAFOOD ON TOP, PRESS IT IN, AND DO NOT TOUCH IT. The prawns and squid get pressed into the batter surface BEFORE the flip. Then patience: leave the pancake completely alone until the edges are crisp and golden and the base lifts cleanly — the single biggest pajeon mistake is flipping too early and tearing the whole thing. When a spatula slides under easily, flip in one confident, whole-pancake motion.\n\nTHE DIPPING SAUCE IS HALF THE FLAVOUR. While the second side cooks, stir the soy + vinegar + chilli flakes + sesame oil together. Slice the cooked pancake into rough diamonds at the table — the traditional way it arrives — and dip every piece into the sauce. Eat it hot, straight from the pan, because pajeon is the rare dish that genuinely only works fresh: the crispness is the entire point, and it fades in minutes.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 20g · Carbs 44g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best straight from the pan while shatter-crisp. Leftover pajeon keeps a day in the fridge but goes soft; re-crisp in a hot dry pan, never the microwave. The dipping sauce keeps a week in a jar.","chefNotes":"Two rules are everything: a THIN cold-water batter (crisp outside, soft in) and NO TOUCHING until the base is golden and lifts cleanly. Lay the spring onions in long strands, not chopped through the batter — they are the structure, not a filling.","pairsWith":"Cold beer or makgeolli (the classic milky rice wine) · Kimchi · A light soup alongside","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Samgyeopsal","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Pajeon literally means 'spring-onion pancake' — haemul just adds the seafood. It is one of Korea's most famous rain-day foods, based on an old folk superstition that pancakes sizzling in oil 'drive away the rain'. Makgeolli, the traditional pale rice wine, is the classic pairing to the point that the combination has its own word: makgeolli-with-pajeon. In Korea the most famous pajeon is the whole-spring-onion version from Jeonju and the coast — and the batter, in an irony the world enjoys, often includes some rice flour, giving it a satisfying chew.","howThisFeels":"The furious sizzle of batter in a hot, oiled pan on a rainy evening — then the first crisp-edged golden diamond, dipped in the tart-soy sauce, shared between friends who all agree the rain can stay.","leftovers":["Re-crisp leftover pajeon slices in a hot dry pan for the crispness to return — then cut into thin strips and toss them through a quick fried rice.","Use leftover pajeon strips as the crunchy topping for a bibimbap or salad, like a Korean-style cracker."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Haemul Pajeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The seafood classic — prawns, squid and whole spring onions in a crispy cold-batter pancake, with the mandatory soy-vinegar chilli dip.","trivia":"The classic is the coastal version: whatever is freshly caught, chopped and dotted over the onions. Prawns and squid are the standard pair; clams are a beloved regional swap.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g spring onions · 50g prawns · 50g squid · 80g cake flour · 20g cornflour · 1 egg · 180ml cold water · soy sauce, vinegar, chilli, sesame for dip · salt · pepper · oil","method":["Lay whole-to-halved spring onion stems in a tight log across a hot oiled pan.","Pour the thin cold-water batter over to seep through and spread to the edges.","Press chopped prawns and squid into the surface; leave completely alone until the base is golden and lifts cleanly.","Flip whole, cook the second side, slice into diamonds.","Dip in the soy-vinegar-chilli-sesame sauce and eat immediately."]},{"name":"Kimchi Pajeon (Budget & Veg)","icon":"💰","diet":["veg"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The budget classic — just kimchi and spring onions, no seafood — and the ferment's tang makes it possibly the most moreish pajeon of all.","trivia":"Kimchi pajeon is the home-standard cheap version: a splash of the kimchi juice goes straight into the batter, and the fermented tang against the crispy onion pancake is a Korean pantry legend.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"50g prawns, chopped"},{"item":"50g squid or calamari rings, chopped"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g kimchi, chopped, plus a splash of its juice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fold the chopped kimchi and a splash of its brine straight into the batter — the juice turns the whole pancake tangy and pink. Lay the spring onion raft, pour the kimchi batter over, and cook exactly as the classic. Skip the egg and use a light vegetable stock for the batter for a fully vegan pancake; the dipping sauce is naturally vegan (soy + vinegar + chilli + sesame)."}]}},{"name":"Quick — The 15-Minute Pan Squared","icon":"⚡","costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The weeknight version — pre-cut fritters instead of one big pancake, so it cooks in half the time and nobody fights over the diamonds.","trivia":"Baking the pajeon as small individual fritters pans faster and needs no flip-courage — the technique is identical at a smaller scale.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Instead of one giant pancake, ladle the batter into 4-5 small rounds in the pan, pressing the onion strands and seafood into each. They cook in about 3 minutes a side and flip with zero risk. Serve the little fritters stacked with the same dipping sauce — same crispness, quarter the flip-nerves."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-doenjang-jjigae","name":"Doenjang Jjigae","nameAlt":"Soybean paste stew — the earthy soul of the Korean table","aliases":["doenjang jjigae","soybean paste stew","doenjang stew","Korean miso stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","soybean","vegan","comfort","one-pot"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","lunch"],"mood":"Grounded & nourishing","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","health":"Genuinely nutritious — fermented soybean paste, vegetables and tofu make this one of the healthiest and most comforting stews in Korean cooking.","allergen":"Soy (doenjang, tofu) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★★ — soybean paste, a zucchini, a potato and tofu; among the cheapest comfort stews there is","audience":"The everyday home stew that Koreans eat for breakfast, lunch or a cold night — the Japanese-umami equivalent of a bowl of miso soup, grown into a full dinner.","season":"A year-round table stead; at its most comforting in winter","ingredients":"30g doenjang (Korean soybean paste — a miso-like fermented paste, darker and earthier than Japanese miso) · 1 knob zucchini (about 80g), cubed · 1 medium potato (about 100g), cubed · 100g firm tofu, cubed · 300ml kelp stock (or water deepened with a strip of kelp) · 1 clove garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · black pepper","method":"DOENJANG IS THE EARTHY SOUL OF THE KOREAN TABLE — the fermented soybean paste that sits beside gochujang in every home, and is the everyday answer to a pot of miso. Where gochujang is fiery and sweet, doenjang is deep, savoury and slightly funky, and a jjigae made from it is Korean comfort at its plainest and most honest. Do not substitute ordinary miso for it without knowing the difference: miso is lighter and sweeter; doenjang is dark, coarse, more assertive — the authentic version is worth a trip to the Asian grocer.\n\n⛔ THE ONE RULE THAT MAKES OR BREAKS IT: DOENJANG IS NEVER BOILED INTO A STEW FROM THE START. Boiling hard destroys its delicate fermented aroma — Koreans say it 'kills' the paste. The correct method is to dissolve the paste into the simmering stock AFTER the other ingredients have cooked, and never let the pot RAPIDLY BOIL again.\n\nBUILD THE VEGETABLE BASE FIRST. In a small pot, heat the sesame oil and fry the garlic and onion for a minute; add the cubed potato and a splash of the stock, and cook gently 5 minutes until the potato begins to soften. Then add the rest of the stock (or the kelp-deepened water) and bring to a GENTLE simmer — bubbling lazily, not boiling hard.\n\n⚖️ NOW THE DOENJANG — DISSOLVED IN A LADLE, NEVER DUMPED IN. Spoon the paste into a ladle or a small sieve, hold it in the simmering broth, and stir until it dissolves fully. Dissolving it this way — rather than plopping it in a lump — is the professional move that makes it smooth and integrated rather than flaky. Taste and adjust: the paste is salty before you add a pinch of anything, so season almost entirely through it.\n\nADD THE ZUCCHINI AND TOFU, AND STOP. The zucchini goes in for the last 8 minutes so it just softens, and the tofu in cubes for the last 5, so it warms but stays intact. After the paste goes in and the zucchini and tofu join, do not let it return to a hard boil — the aroma of the paste is what this dish is about.\n\nFINISH with the spring onion and a thread of sesame oil off the heat. Serve immediately with a bowl of steamed rice — the classic Korean alternation of a spoonful of stew, then a bite of plain rice, keeps the earthy depth singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 40g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Doenjang jjigae keeps 3 days in the fridge and genuinely deepens as it rests — reheat gently, never boil. Freezes well for a month. The tofu and zucchini soften further, so refresh them when reheating a frozen portion.","chefNotes":"The one rule to remember: do not boil the paste hard. The vegetable base cooks first, the paste dissolves in the simmering liquid through a ladle, and the pot never returns to a hard boil after — that gentle treatment is what keeps the deep fermented aroma that defines the dish.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · Fresh kimchi · A quick cucumber or radish banchan · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Doenjang is Korea's everyday fermented soybean paste — a sibling of Japanese miso, but darker, chunkier and more intensely savoury, fermented in the traditional way in clay jars that bury the block in brine. It is one of the pillars of the Korean banchan table, and doenjang-jjigae is widely regarded as the Korean 'miso soup grown into a meal'. The family jjigae pot is such a fixture of Korean home cooking that it has spawned a national saying: 'the soup pot is the heart of the home'.","howThisFeels":"A rustic, earthy, deeply comforting bowl — the dark broth glistening, the zucchini just giving, and the subtle fermented depth that tastes like Korea itself rather than any single ingredient.","leftovers":["The day-two jjigae is even deeper — reheat gently and it is the classic Korean cold-weather lunch.","Spoon the leftover stew over freshly steamed rice topped with a fried egg for an honest jjigae-donburi.","Freeze on its own; the paste-based broth is very freezable and 5 minutes of reheating with fresh tofu rebuilds the pot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Doenjang Jjigae (Vegan)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The classic bowl — potato, zucchini and tofu in the earthy paste broth, naturally fully vegan and one of Korea's healthiest comfort stews.","trivia":"The classic is vegan even in Korea — tofu, vegetables and soybean paste, no meat needed. The deep umami comes entirely from the fermentation of the paste and the kelp stock.","delta":{},"ingredients":"30g doenjang · 80g zucchini · 100g potato · 100g firm tofu · 300ml kelp stock · 1 clove garlic · 30g onion · 1 spring onion · 5ml sesame oil · pepper","method":["Fry garlic and onion in sesame oil; add cubed potato and a splash of stock; cook 5 minutes.","Add the rest of the stock; bring to a gentle simmer.","Dissolve the doenjang through a ladle into the simmering broth — never let the pot boil hard after.","Add the zucchini and tofu for the last 8 minutes and 5 minutes respectively.","Finish with spring onion and sesame off the heat; serve with rice."]},{"name":"Doenjang Jjigae with Clams (The Classic in Korea)","icon":"🍲","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The most common Korean home version — clams add a clean mineral sweetness that the earthy paste loves.","trivia":"Clams (or mussels) are the most traditional add-in to doenjang-jjigae in Korean homes — the clean ocean sweetness against the deep soybean funk is a classic matchup. A little seafood stock with the clams deepens it further.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g fresh clams or mussels, well-scrubbed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the scrubbed clams with the stock, after the paste has dissolved. Simmer until they just open — 5-7 minutes — then lift them out to serve on top, or leave them in the pot. The broth they release is one of the best things about this version. Discard any that do not open."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Doenjang","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"The fastest honest version — pre-tinned or frozen vegetables and bottled stock shrink the classic to a genuine quarter-hour bowl.","trivia":"A quality bottled kelp stock and pre-cubed vegetables make the authentic dish nearly instant — the only rule that must survive any shortcut is the no-hard-boil treatment of the paste.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a good bottled kelp or vegetable stock, tinned (drained) beans or frozen zucchini, and pre-cubed tofu. With the vegetables already cooked, the whole bowl is: gentle simmer, dissolve the paste through a ladle, add the tofu, off the heat. Fifteen minutes, complete, and the paste's aroma intact."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-galbi","name":"Galbi (Korean BBQ Short Ribs)","nameAlt":"Charcoal-grilled marinated short ribs — the heart of Korean barbecue","aliases":["galbi","korean short ribs","kalbi","la galbi","beef short ribs"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","grill","bbq","celebratory"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","family","bbq","special"],"mood":"Festive & celebratory","time":"Prep 20 min · Marinate at least 3 h (ideally overnight) · Grill 5-6 min total","health":"Special-occasion rich — short ribs are not an everyday cut, and the marinade is sweet. Savour the centrepiece rather than scale it down.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · pear (optional tenderiser)","budget":"★☆☆ — short ribs are the special-tier cut; the rest of the marinade is pantry staples","audience":"The celebration platter — the dish Koreans serve when it must be good, at holidays, birthdays and gatherings.","season":"Any celebration; autumn and winter gatherings are the classic home","ingredients":"400g beef short ribs (flanken-cut, LA-style: thin slices across the bones — ask your butcher) · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear or apple juice (the quiet tenderiser) · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 15ml mirin or Korean cooking wine · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 1 pear halved (for the pan-side char — optional) · black pepper","method":"⚖️ GALBI IS THE KING OF KOREAN BARBECUE — and the cut is everything. The classic uses LA-style (flanken) short ribs: thin strips cut ACROSS the bones, each piece a ribbon of meat with three or four little round bone discs showing. This cut matters because the thin, bone-with-meat slices cook fast, char beautifully, and are eaten by wrapping small bundles in lettuce or eating as they come. Ask your butcher to cut the ribs flanken-style (about 1cm thick, across the bone) — getting the right cut is 70% of a great galbi.\n\nTHE MARINADE IS THE SECOND HALF — AND THE PEAR IS THE SECRET. Whisk the soy, pear juice, honey, mirin, sesame oil, garlic and ginger together. The pear's enzymes (papain-family) tenderise the meat exactly like they do in bulgogi, and its natural sugars are what caramelise into that lacquered, glossy char. Thirty minutes of marinating is the floor; overnight is the peak — galbi is one of the few dishes where the difference between 30 minutes and 8 hours is genuinely night and day.\n\n⚖️ THE GRILL, AND THE GRILL'S ONE RULE: DO NOT LET THE SUGAR BURN. The glaze's sugars are what make galbi taste so good and what make it burn so easily. Grill over MEDIUM heat — glowing coals rather than open flame — and turn the ribs frequently, around every 30 seconds, so the sugars caramelise into lacquer rather than scorch into bitter char. Watch the drips: if the fat flares and billows black smoke, move the ribs to a cooler zone immediately.\n\nTHE TRADITIONAL FLIP: many Korean cooks place a halved pear, cut-side down, on the grill beside the ribs and use it to press the meat — the pear's juice keeps the surface moist and adds a whisper of fruit. It is old-school theatre and genuinely good.\n\nREST THE RIB RIBBONS ONE MINUTE, CUT ONCE, AND EAT. Cut each ribbon into two pieces at the table (scissors, the Korean way), so each piece keeps its bone handle. Serve with lettuce or perilla leaves, ssamjang, raw garlic and sliced chilli for the classic wrap — and steamed rice on the side, because galbi without rice is a smile without the reason.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Marinate 3 h–overnight · Grill 5-6 min total","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 20g · Fat 36g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"The uncooked marinated ribs keep 2 days in the fridge and freeze beautifully for a month — the marinade protects the meat. Cooked galbi keeps 3 days, is superb cold, and famously improves into tomorrow's fried rice or a galbi-jjim-style braise.","chefNotes":"The flanken cross-bone cut is non-negotiable, the pear is the quiet star, and the grill must run MEDIUM with frequent turns — the sugar glaze is the flavour and the enemy. Rest the ribbons, cut with scissors, wrap in lettuce.","pairsWith":"Lettuce or perilla wraps with ssamjang, raw garlic and chilli · Steamed rice · Cold soju or beer","goesWith":["Samgyeopsal","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Galbi means 'rib' — and the LA-style cut owes its existence to Korean immigrants in Los Angeles, who adapted the traditional long Korean cut to the flanken style butchers there already knew. It was the dish that built many a Korean-American restaurant. At home in Korea, galbi is the celebratory meat of Korean barbecue: often served as a whole long bone at the grill and cut at the table with scissors, and so tied to gathering that a 'galbi day' with family is considered the perfect weekend.","howThisFeels":"The lacquered, glossy sheen of a properly grilled rib, the smoke rising off the coals, and the first bite — charred and caramelised at the edge, pear-tender in the middle — wrapped in cool lettuce with a sting of garlic.","leftovers":["Chop leftover galbi off the bones and stir it through cold-day-old rice with a spoon of gochujang — galbi fried rice, the family day-two favourite.","Make galbi-jjim: braise leftover ribs with soy-sweet broth, potato and carrot for a slow-cooked second meal.","Freeze cooked galbi in the marinade-juice; thaw and reheat gently in a pan."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"LA Galbi (Flanken Short Ribs)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":62,"howThisFeels":"The classic — flanken-cut short ribs, pear-soy marinade, lacquered over a medium grill, eaten in lettuce wraps.","trivia":"The LA-style flanken cut across the bones is the modern standard and the reason the dish cooks so fast: each thin slice carries its own little bone handles and chars beautifully.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g flanken-cut short ribs · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear juice · 15ml honey · 15ml mirin · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · pear half for the grill (optional) · pepper","method":["Whisk the soy, pear, honey, mirin, sesame, garlic and ginger; marinate the flanken ribs 3 hours to overnight.","Grill over MEDIUM heat, turning every 30 seconds so the sugars lacquer rather than burn.","Press the cut-side of a halved pear onto the meat as it grills (traditional and good).","Rest 1 minute, cut each ribbon once with scissors, wrap in lettuce with ssamjang and garlic.","Serve with steamed rice and cold soju."]},{"name":"Chicken Galbi (Dak Galbi — Budget)","icon":"🐔","costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same pear-soy lacquer on chicken thigh — juicier, cheaper, and the most popular weeknight barbecue substitute in Korea itself.","trivia":"Dak galbi (chicken ribs) uses boneless chicken thigh with the same marinade — the thigh's fat keeps it silky where breast dries out. It is the budget barbecue hero.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400g beef short ribs, flanken-cut","to":"400g chicken thigh, boneless, in thin medallions"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Chicken needs a full grill to cook through — give each medal 2 minutes a side over medium heat rather than the fast beef char. Same marinade, same lacquer, same lettuce-wrap table."}]}},{"name":"Grilled Mushroom & Pear Galbi-Ssam (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The galbi ritual, given over to the grill — big mushroom caps basted in the same pear-soy glaze, wrapped with raw garlic in lettuce. A vegan centrepiece the table argues over.","trivia":"The ssam ritual was never about the meat — char-grilled mushrooms with the galbi marinade, wrapped with garlic and chilli, are a beloved Korean barbecue vegetable order, and the marinade's pear sweetness becomes a glaze on the mushrooms.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g beef short ribs, flanken-cut"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g large portobello or shiitake mushrooms, thicksliced"},{"item":"extra sesame oil for the marinade brush"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Marinate the thick-sliced mushrooms in the same galbi soy-pear glaze for 20 minutes, then grill over medium heat until deeply lacquered and tender — about 3 minutes a side. Serve exactly as the classic: lettuce wraps, ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli. The marinade is naturally vegan; it just needs mushrooms to star."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-bossam","name":"Bossam","nameAlt":"Boiled pork belly wraps — the laid-back party feast","aliases":["bossam","boiled pork belly","korean pork wraps","suyuk"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","no-fry","slow","celebratory","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","celebration","casual","family"],"mood":"Relaxed & festive","time":"Prep 15 min · Boil & rest 2 h 30 min","health":"Far leaner than a fried pork — boiled belly sheds much of its fat in the cooking liquid; the garlic and greens keep it balanced.","allergen":"Soy (ssamjang) · sesame · mustard (in the classic ssam table) · gluten (soy sauce)","budget":"★★☆ — a belly roast is affordable for a crowd; it is one of Korea's great budget-feast centrepieces","audience":"The no-fuss party dish — slow-boiled, sliced, and everyone wraps their own at the table.","season":"Any gathering; a favourite at holidays and family dinners","ingredients":"400g pork belly, in one solid slab · 4 cloves garlic · 1-2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions, chopped · 1 tbsp doenjang or a splash of soju (optional but traditional) · 20g cabbage leaves or lettuce · 20g ssamjang (or mix from gochujang + doenjang + sesame + garlic) · 8 fresh oysters (optional, the classic luxury) · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced · 1 green chilli, sliced · a little radish pickle (danmuji) · black pepper","method":"⚖️ BOSSAM IS THE GREAT UN-FANCY FEAST — a whole slab of pork belly boiled with aromatics until meltingly tender, sliced thick, and served with a live 'ssam table' of pickles, garlic, greens and, classically, raw oysters. It is the party food that asks for almost no attention and delivers a table everyone builds differently.\n\nBOIL THE BELLY LOW AND LONG — THIS IS THE WHOLE TECHNIQUE. Place the whole slab in a pot, cover with cold water, and bring to a bare simmer. Skim the scum that rises in the first ten minutes (it will cloud the broth), then add the garlic, ginger, spring onions and a spoon of doenjang or a splash of soju — both optional but traditional, adding a quiet savour-note to the pork as it cooks. Simmer gently, never boiling hard, for about 2 hours — the pork should be so tender that a chopstick slides through with no resistance. The slow simmer is the secret; a hard boil gives tough, dense belly instead of soft, cake-like slices.\n\nREST 10 MINUTES, THEN SLICE THICK — the rest is as important here as for a roast, giving the juices time to settle before the belly is cut into thick, 1cm slices, each one a little tower of clean pork. Serve the slices in a pile, still warm.\n\n⚖️ THE SSAM TABLE IS THE SECOND HALF — AND THE OYSTER IS THE CLASSIC SURPRISE. Around the pork go: crisp cabbage or lettuce leaves, ssamjang, sliced raw garlic, green chilli rings, the radish pickle, and — if you can — fresh oysters. The classic bossam wrap is a cabbage leaf loaded with pork, cold raw oyster, garlic, a swipe of ssamjang and a chilli ring, folded into one full bite. The briny cold oyster against the warm clean pork is one of Korea's great taste collisions, and why bossam is considered a special-occasion dish.\n\nEVERYONE WRAPS THEIR OWN — that is the form. No plates needed beyond a leaf and a lap, and the table does the work. Steamed rice and a simple cold soup complete the spread.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Boil & rest 2 h 30 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 8g · Fat 42g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Cooked bossam keeps 3 days in the fridge, and is famously spectacular cold — the traditional bossam drinking snack is cold slices with garlic. Reheat gently in a steamer, or eat cold. The boiled belly also freezes for a month.","chefNotes":"Low and slow is the whole technique — a bare simmer, never a boil, with the aromatics lending quiet depth. Rest before slicing, slice thick, and let the oyster-garlic-ssamjang wrap do the rest. The table is the recipe.","pairsWith":"Fresh oysters (the classic luxury) · Ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli · Radish kimchi or pickle · Cold beer or soju","goesWith":["Samgyeopsal","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bossam means 'wrapped' — from the Korean verb to wrap, capturing the dish's entire character. It is a party dish sometimes translated as 'boiled pork belly wraps', and in Korea it is a favourite at holidays and family tables for being genuinely low-effort: the boil does the work, and the wow is in the table of accompaniments rather than any complex technique. The cooked pork is also the basis of the 'bossam kimchi' specialty — cabbage leaves stuffed with seasoned minced pork, a celebrated Korean side dish in its own right.","howThisFeels":"The long slow simmer filling the kitchen with gentle pork scent, then the reveal: thick, tender slices of white-clean pork in a stack, the oyster-garlic-chilli table around it, and the first wrap that fuses warm clean meat with cold briny oyster — a bite that makes the table go quiet.","leftovers":["Eat the cold slices as a drinking snack with garlic and ssamjang — the most authentic day-two move there is.","Shred leftover bossam through kimchi fried rice or over a bowl of rice with the pickles.","Use the leftover cooking liquid as a rich soup base — add kimchi and you have kimchi jjigae in minutes."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bossam with Oyster","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The full spectacle — slow-boiled whole belly, thick slices, the oyster-garlic-ssamjang table.","trivia":"The oyster is the classic that makes it a celebration — the raw cold oyster in the warm pork wrap is one of Korea's great taste moments.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g pork belly slab · 4 cloves garlic · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1 tbsp doenjang · cabbage leaves · 20g ssamjang · 8 fresh oysters (optional) · 2 cloves raw garlic · 1 green chilli · radish pickle · pepper","method":["Simmer the whole belly slab in cold water with garlic, ginger, spring onion and a spoon of doenjang for 2 hours until chopstick-tender.","Rest 10 minutes, slice into thick 1cm towers.","Set the ssam table: cabbage leaves, ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli, radish pickle, fresh oysters.","Wrap: leaf, warm pork, cold raw oyster, garlic, chilli, ssamjang — one full bite.","Repeat until the table is happy."]},{"name":"Budget Bossam — No Oysters & Quicker Boil","icon":"💰","costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same slow-simmered belly, no oysters, cooking time somewhat shrunk with a faster cut — every bit the party centrepiece without the luxury add-on.","trivia":"The oysters are the luxury, not the essence — bossam without them is still a beloved Korean party dish, with the garlic, chilli and ssamjang wrap doing all the work.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8 fresh oysters (optional, the classic luxury)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Without the oysters, the wrap is still complete: warm pork, strong garlic, chilli and the ssamjang is the everyday bossam — and if you are short on time, a 1kg piece cooks fine in 1.5-1.75 hours as long as it stays at a bare simmer."}]}},{"name":"Tofu Bossam (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"Pan-crisped tofu slabs taking the centre of the same ssam table — a vegan bossam that keeps the wrap ritual and its bright table.","trivia":"Korean temple cooking has always done exactly this — firm tofu, crisped in the pan, wrapped with the bright garlic-and-pickle table. The wrap is the point, and tofu wears it beautifully.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g pork belly, in one solid slab"}],"addIng":[{"item":"300g extra-firm tofu, pressed and thick-sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu, slice into thick slabs, and pan-fry in a little sesame oil until deeply golden and crisp on both sides. Serve as the centre of the same ssam table — cabbage leaves, ssamjang (use a maple-sweetened gochujang-doenjang mix for full vegan), garlic, chilli, pickle. The wrap ritual survives completely healthily."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-naengmyeon","name":"Mul Naengmyeon","nameAlt":"Cold buckwheat noodles in icy broth — Korea's heat-beating summer bowl","aliases":["naengmyeon","mul naengmyeon","cold noodles","korean cold buckwheat noodles"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","cold","summer","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["summer","everyday","lunch","refresh"],"mood":"Refreshing & clarifying","time":"Prep 10 min (+chill 1 h) · Cook 5 min","health":"Genuinely light and refreshing — buckwheat noodles, icy broth, shredded vegetables and protein; a hot-day bowl that is a full meal without the heaviness.","allergen":"Buckwheat (in the noodles) · soy (soy, gochujang or mustard in the seasoning) · egg (classic garnish) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — noodles and broth, shredded vegetables and an egg; one of the cheapest peaks in Korean summer eating","audience":"The summer-rescue bowl — served icy cold, it is Korea's answer to the heatwave, and the single dish that defines Korean summer food.","season":"SUMMER — unambiguously. It is eaten when the temperature demands ice in the broth, and barely makes sense in winter.","ingredients":"100g mul naengmyeon noodles (Korean cold buckwheat noodles — buckwheat with a little starch; the greyish-brown kind) · 1 egg, hard-boiled · 50g cucumber, cut into fine matchsticks · 40g Korean pear or apple, cut into matchsticks · 1 tsp mustard (or gochujang + vinegar for the red version) · 1 tbsp rice vinegar · 5ml soy sauce · 1 tsp sugar · 10ml ice-cold dongchimi radish water, or iced broth made from kelp + water, well-chilled · ice cubes · toasted sesame seeds","method":"⚖️ NAENGMYEON IS KOREA'S DEFINING SUMMER BOWL — a tangle of cold buckwheat noodles in an ice-cold broth, sweetened and sharpened, eaten when the mercury decides brute force is the only response. The two things that make it work: the broth must be genuinely icy, and the noodles must be chewy, not soft.\n\nTHE BROTH IS THE SOUL. The classic is dongchimi — the clear, tart, chilled brine of winter-radish kimchi — or a light kelp-and-water broth chilled to icy. For the honest home version: steep a strip of kelp in cold water for 30 minutes, add the rice vinegar, soy, sugar, and a little mustard (or a spoon of gochujang for the red version), and chill it thoroughly. Whatever you use, the broth must be BRISKLY COLD — traditional naengmyeon is served with ice cubes floating in it, and the iciness is not decorative, it is the dish.\n\nTHE NOODLES ARE CHEWY — because they are buckwheat and starch, and that pairing is the whole texture. Cook the noodles per the packet (usually 3-4 minutes for the thin grey-brown mul naengmyeon kind), then the secret step: rinse them under cold running water while rubbing gently with your hands, then drain well — this stops the cooking instantly AND washes off the excess starch, leaving each strand slippery and separate, not clumped. Then give them one final swish in iced water to chill through.\n\nTANGLE, GARNISH, AND FREEZE AS YOU EAT. Coil the cold noodles high in the middle of the bowl like a nest, pour the icy broth around, and crown with the cucumber and pear matchsticks, half the boiled egg, a thread of mustard (or gochujang-vinegar), and sesame seeds. Add the ice cubes. Eat immediately, using scissors to cut the long noodles if they resist — cold noodles always fight back a little, and Korean tables keep scissors on hand for exactly this.\n\n⚖️ THE KOREAN RULE: EAT IT FAST. A naengmyeon is at its peak for about fifteen minutes, before the broth warms and the noodles slacken. It is a dish to be devoured, not savoured slowly.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min (+chill 1 h) · Cook 5 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 62g · Fat 8g · Sodium ~800mg","storage":"Best eaten immediately at its icy peak. The chilled seasoned broth keeps 2 days refrigerated — re-chill it hard before use. Cooked noodles keep a day rinsed in cold water and drained; they are served cold the next day, no reheating, with a fresh pour of icy broth.","chefNotes":"The broth must be BRISKLY cold — ice cubes are part of the serving, not a garnish. Rinse the cooked noodles under cold water and give them a final iced swish so each strand stays separate and chewy. Eat immediately at its icy peak.","pairsWith":"A little mustard or gochujang at the table · Hard-boiled egg and pear for sweetness · The coldest room in the house","goesWith":["Gimbap","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Naengmyeon means 'cold noodles', and in Korea it is the un-negotiable food of summer — restaurants advertise 'mul naengmyeon' with the kind of chill-marketing other nations give ice cream. It originated in the northern Korean provinces of Pyeongyang and Hamhung, where the cold climate and the buckwheat fields made cold noodle broths a regional staple long before they became the nationwide heat-beater. The tradition of eating long noodles is also tied to birthday wishes for long life, which is why naengmyeon, despite being the lightest dish of the Korean table, is one of the foods most associated with celebration.","howThisFeels":"The first shock of genuinely icy broth, the chewy-slippery tangle of buckwheat noodles, and the sweet clean crunch of pear and cucumber between mouthfuls — the single most refreshing thing on the Korean table on a brutal summer day.","leftovers":["The cooked, rinsed noodles keep a day cold — serve tomorrow with a fresh icy broth pour, no reheating.","The seasoned broth keeps 2 days chilled; it is the base for a fast second bowl with fresh noodles.","Leftover garnishes (cucumber, egg, pear) become a fresh salad bowl with sesame."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mul Naengmyeon (Icy Broth)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The classic — buckwheat noodle nest in genuinely icy broth, with cucumber, pear and a boiled egg, mustard on the side.","trivia":"The classic is the Pyeongyang-style mul naengmyeon: icy broth, chewy buckwheat noodles, and the sweet-salty balance of pear and pickle.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g mul naengmyeon noodles · 1 egg · 50g cucumber · 40g pear · mustard · rice vinegar · soy · sugar · ice-cold kelp/radish broth · ice cubes · sesame seeds","method":["Chill the seasoned kelp broth hard (ice cubes in the bowl at the end).","Cook the noodles 3-4 min, rinse in cold water rubbing gently, drain, and swish in iced water.","Coil the cold noodles high like a nest, pour icy broth around, garnish with cucumber, pear, halved boiled egg, mustard, sesame.","Add ice cubes and eat immediately — a naengmyeon lives about fifteen minutes."]},{"name":"Bibim Naengmyeon (Spicy Red, No Broth)","icon":"🌶️","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The Hamhung-style fiery twin — the same chewy cold noodles tossed in a cold gochujang sauce with no broth, more pike, more kick.","trivia":"Hamhung-style bibim naengmyeon is famously spicy — seasoned with gochujang, garlic and a sharp cold sauce, and unlike its mul cousin, served without broth. It is Korea's heat-on-heat answer to a summer day.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"For the red broth-less version: toss the cold rinsed noodles in a cold sauce of gochujang, rice vinegar, soy, sugar, garlic and a thread of sesame oil, then garnish with the cucumber, pear, egg and sesame. Serve with a small side cup of cold water or broth to sip — the classic bibim naengmyeon side. For a fully vegan bowl, skip the egg and use a gochujang-sugar sauce; the rest is naturally plant-based."}]}}]},{"id":"korea-yukgaejang","name":"Yukgaejang","nameAlt":"Spicy beef & vegetable soup — the bone-cleansing fire bowl","aliases":["yukgaejang","spicy beef soup","korean beef vegetable soup","yukkaejang"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","spicy","comfort","winter","restorative"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","restorative"],"mood":"Fiery & restorative","time":"Prep 15 min · Simmer 1 h 45 min","health":"A genuinely restorative bowl — lean beef and vegetables in a fiery, garlicky broth. The heat is real purpose: this is the dish Koreans reach for when they feel a cold arriving.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★☆ — beef brisket is the main cost; the vegetables are cheap and the broth is pantry-built","audience":"The restorative weekday soup — Korea's answer to the pot of soup that heals whatever has you down.","season":"Winter, cold nights, and the first shiver of a cold — though its fans will eat it in summer rain without blinking","ingredients":"150g beef brisket or shin, in a chunk · 30g gosari (dried fernbrake — see note; or rehydrated mushrooms) · 25g doraji (dried bellflower root — optional; or extra bean sprouts) · 60g bean sprouts · 100g spring onions (a whole generous bunch — this soup is a bed of them) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 slice ginger · 1 egg (for the classic yolk-and-white ribbons) · black pepper · salt","method":"YUKGAEJANG IS KOREA'S FIREY RESTORATIVE — a bowl that tastes the way a thermos feels before a long winter walk. Its literal name carries its purpose: yukgaejang is said to mean 'beef soup served to someone who has suffered', and it is the soup Koreans cook for themselves when a cold is circling. IT IS BUILT IN TWO CHAPTERS — a long quiet simmer that makes the beef shreddable, then a fiery 30-minute finish that builds the broth.\n\nFIRST, THE DRIED MOUNTAIN VEGETABLES. Traditional yukgaejang is defined by two wild ingredients the global reader may not know: gosari (dried fernbrake — rehydrated it is chewy and earthy, the soul of the soup's texture) and doraji (dried bellflower root — a crisp, slightly bitter root like a wild parsnip). Both are sold at Asian grocers and are worth hunting for once. The honest global swaps: gosari → any rehydrated mushroom (shiitake or wood ear); doraji → an extra handful of bean sprouts. Soak the gosari and doraji in cold water 2 hours (or quick-soak in hot water 30 minutes), then simmer them 20 minutes in fresh water to soften before they join the soup.\n\n⚖️ CHAPTER ONE — THE QUIET SIMMER THAT MAKES THE BEEF. Put the beef in a pot with cold water to cover, the ginger, a few of the spring onion whites, and bring to a boil. Skim the scum that rises, then lower to a bare simmer for 1 hour 15 minutes — the beef is done when a chopstick slides through and it can be shredded by hand. Lift the beef out, let it cool, and SHRED it into rough strands — the shredded beef is the texture of the finished bowl, never cubed meat. Strain the broth and keep every drop.\n\n⚖️ CHAPTER TWO — THE FIRE. Heat the sesame oil in the same pot, add the gochugaru and garlic, and stir 30 seconds until the flakes bloom into a deep red oil. Add the shredded beef, the softened gosari and doraji (or their swaps), the soy, and a good splash of the broth, and let the whole thing come together over medium heat — this is where the soup gains its colour and its bones.\n\nADD THE REMAINING BROTH AND THE BEAN SPROUTS, and simmer 25-30 minutes until the broth is deeply red and glossy. Season with salt and black pepper — the soup should be savoury and fiery, with the beef tasting of itself.\n\n⚖️ THE SIGNATURE: THE SPRING ONION BED AND THE EGG RIBBONS. A proper yukgaejang carries a MOUND of spring onions — sliced thick and stirred through for the last 5 minutes so they stay a little toothsome. And the classic garnish is a separate pan-beaten egg, cooked into a thin sheet and sliced into white and yellow ribbons, scattered over the top like a ribbon in a gift. Serve with steamed rice, and alternate spoonfuls of soup and bites of plain rice as the heat demands.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Simmer 1 h 45 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 12g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Famously better on day two — the beef drinks the broth and stays tender. Keeps 4 days in the fridge and freezes perfectly for 2 months; it is one of the best freezers in Korean cooking. Reheat gently — the beef can go from silky to stringy with a hard boil.","chefNotes":"Two chapters, never one: the long quiet simmer that makes the beef shreddable, then the fiery finish that builds the broth. The gochugaru is bloomed in oil first — that step is the difference between a fire bowl and a watery chilli soup. And the spring onions are not a garnish; they are a whole bed of texture.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice · Kimchi · A cold pickled radish side · Cold barley tea between spoonfuls","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Doenjang Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Yukgaejang's name is its story — 'beef soup served to someone who has suffered', which is why it is Korea's designated restorative. The dried mountain vegetables give it its particular chewiness: gosari (fernbrake) and doraji (bellflower root) are foraged wild plants, dried for the pantry, and they are the reason the soup is as much about texture as taste. In Korean restaurants it is served in a sizzling stone bowl that keeps bubbling when it reaches the table, and it has a long-standing reputation as the hangover cure and the cold-season fix in equal measure.","howThisFeels":"The deep red glossy broth, the shreds of tender beef, and that first fiery spoonful that warms from the stomach outwards — the spring onion and egg ribbon finish making it a bowl that somehow tastes both powerful and comforting.","leftovers":["Day-two yukgaejang is a matter of pride — reheat gently and the broth is deeper, the beef softer.","Serve the leftover soup over freshly cooked noodles for a fiery yukgaejang-guksu the next day.","The leftover broth alone is gold: freeze it and use it as the stock base for a kimchi-jjigae."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic with Gosari & Doraji","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The complete restorative — shredded beef, chewy fernbrake, crisp root, and the fire bowl of spring onions and egg ribbons.","trivia":"The classic is the foraged-vegetable version — gosari and doraji give the soup its signature chew, and the egg ribbons the restaurant finish.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g beef brisket · 30g gosari (soaked) · 25g doraji (soaked) · 60g bean sprouts · 100g spring onions · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 1 slice ginger · 1 egg · pepper · salt","method":["Soak the gosari and doraji; simmer 20 minutes in fresh water until softened.","Simmer the beef in cold water with ginger and spring onion whites for 1 h 15 min until shreddable; shred it; keep the broth.","Bloom gochugaru and garlic in sesame oil 30 seconds; add beef, vegetables, soy and broth.","Simmer 25-30 min until deep red and glossy; season.","Stir in the spring onion mound for the last 5 min; finish with egg ribbons. Serve with rice."]},{"name":"Quick Pressure-Cooker Yukgaejang","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same fire bowl in roughly half the time — a pressure cooker does Chapter One in 30 minutes instead of 75.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely transforms this soup: the beef reaches shreddable tenderness in 30 minutes, and the broth gains depth faster because the pressure pushes flavour in. Everything after the simmer is identical.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Pressure-cook the beef with the ginger, aromatics and water for 30-40 minutes until fork-tender, then shred. Bloom the gochugaru in the open pot and follow Chapter Two exactly — the whole bowl comes together in about 1 hour with no sacrifice. Pre-sliced stir-fry beef can even skip the long simmer entirely: use it shreddable-cooked in 5 minutes, at the cost of a lighter broth."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian Yukgaejang (Mushroom)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The same fiery red broth and the same mountain-vegetable chew, with mushrooms and tofu standing in for the beef — a vegetarian soup that keeps the fire alive.","trivia":"The gosari and doraji are vegetables already — a vegetarian yukgaejang simply swaps the beef for a mix of shiitake, oyster mushrooms and tofu, and deepens the broth with kelp and mushroom stock. The temple kitchen tradition of Korean vegetarian cooking has always kept the spice.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g beef brisket or shin, in a chunk"}],"addIng":[{"item":"120g mixed mushrooms (shiitake, oyster, button), sliced"},{"item":"100g firm tofu, cubed"},{"item":"kelp and shiitake stock in place of beef broth"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the sliced mushrooms hard in the sesame oil before the gochugaru blooms — they carry the savour the beef would have lent. Use a deep kelp-and-shiitake stock for the broth, skip the ginger beef simmer, and add the tofu in the last 5 minutes so it warms through. Everything else — the fire, the spring onions, the egg ribbons (skip for vegan) — stands exactly as the classic."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-samgyetang","name":"Samgyetang","nameAlt":"Ginseng chicken soup — the whole-chicken summer health bowl","aliases":["samgyetang","ginseng chicken soup","korean ginseng soup","samgye-tang"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","chicken","whole-chicken","health","celebration"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["health","summer","celebration","special"],"mood":"Restorative & nurturing","time":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 15 min","health":"The legendary Korean health soup — whole chicken stuffed with glutinous rice and ginseng, eaten as a restorative. The ginseng is the point: warming, grounding, the traditional cure for summer heat-exhaustion.","allergen":"Ginseng (the herb) · chicken · rice","budget":"★★☆ — a small whole chicken is the cost; ginseng is a pantry hero you use a pinch of","audience":"The special health meal — one whole chicken per person at its most traditional, or one big bird shared for two.","season":"The famous summer bowl — eaten on the three sam-bok days (the hottest days of the Korean summer) as a warm-you-in-the-heat cure","ingredients":"1 small whole chicken (about 600-800g — corn-fed or small broiler) · 60g glutinous rice (sweet rice — soaked 30 minutes) · 1 medium ginseng root (or 1 tsp ginseng powder, or 2 sachets ginseng tea) · 4 cloves garlic · 3 red dates (jujube — optional but traditional) · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1L water · salt & pepper · optional: a few chestnuts or ginkgo nuts if you find them","method":"⚖️ SAMGYETANG IS KOREA'S SUMMER MEDICINE — a whole small chicken stuffed with glutinous rice, garlic and a ginseng root, simmered until it is so tender it sheds its own meat, served as a milky soup. It is the dish Koreans eat on the hottest three days of summer (sam-bok) — the logic being that heat inside beats heat outside, and the ginseng plus chicken restores what summer drains. It IS medicine, prepared as food.\n\n⚖️ THE STUFFING IS THE SOUL — AND OVERSTUFFING IS THE SIN. Soak the glutinous rice 30 minutes first; it doubles as it cooks, so the cavity takes only about two-thirds full. Into the cavity go the drained rice, the ginseng root (or a generous pinch of ginseng powder — the root's strong earthy-bitter heart), the garlic cloves, the jujube and a slice of ginger. Truss the chicken closed with kitchen string, or simply tuck the legs to hold it — the trussing keeps the rice from boiling out.\n\nSET THE CHICKEN IN A POT, BREAST UP, AND COVER WITH COLD WATER. Add the remaining ginger and a spring onion. Bring to a boil, skim the scum thoroughly, and drop to the gentlest simmer — the pot should just barely tremble. Simmer 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes, turning the chicken once at the halfway point. The chicken is done when it is fall-apart tender and the broth has turned milky white — that milky transformation is the collagen emulsifying into the water, and it is the mark of a properly long, gentle simmer.\n\n⚖️ THE BROTH IS THE MEDICINE — SEASON AT THE TABLE, NOT IN THE POT. A samgyetang broth is barely seasoned: a little salt and pepper to taste at the table, because the chicken, rice and ginseng carry the whole flavour. The ritual is to eat the chicken (falling off the bone, lifting clean with chopsticks), then the rice that has cooked inside it into a gentle porridge, then drink the milky broth like a warm tonic.\n\nSERVE IN THE POT OR A LARGE BOWL, WHOLE, WITH THE SALT AND PEPPER BESIDE IT. This is a one-chicken-per-person dish at its most traditional; one large bird shared between two is the honest practical version, with rice on the side.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 15 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g · Carbs 30g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~600mg","storage":"Best the day it is made — the chicken is at its falling-apart peak. Keeps 2 days in the fridge; the broth sets to a natural jelly (a sign of a good long simmer) and re-liquefies on gentle reheating. The stuffed rice swallows the broth and becomes a porridge — reheat gently and add a splash of water.","chefNotes":"Three rules: soak the rice, never overstuff (it doubles), and SKIM, then simmer gently until the broth turns milky white — that milky turn is the collagen, which is the whole point. Season only at the table: the ginseng, chicken and rice are the flavour.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the classic counter to the warm, gentle soup) · Salt & pepper at the table · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Gimbap","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Samgyetang is Korea's most famous seasonal health food — eaten specifically on the sam-bok days, the three hottest days of the lunar summer calendar, when Koreans flock to 'samgyetang restaurants' that open early and sell out by lunch. The logic is traditional Korean medicine: you fight summer heat with warming food that makes you sweat, expelling the heat from within. The ginseng root in the cavity is the active ingredient — Korea's great medicinal herb, prized for restoring energy — and the milky broth is the proof of hours of patient simmering. In full tradition, one whole chicken IS one serving, and the biggest samgyetang chains serve hundreds of birds a day during the three hot days.","howThisFeels":"The whole chicken arriving in its milky broth, so tender the meat lifts off the bones, with the ginseng-earthy steam rising — the warm, grounding feeling of being fed like medicine, bite after bite.","leftovers":["The next-morning move is the best part in disguise: the broth and the rice-porridge filling reheat into a gentle congee — add a splash of water and a little sesame oil.","Shred any leftover chicken into the reheated broth with the remaining porridge for a chicken-juk (congee) breakfast.","Freeze the broth alone; it is a gold-standard stock for any Korean soup."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Whole-Chicken Samgyetang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — one whole stuffed chicken, milky ginseng broth, salt and pepper at the table.","trivia":"The classic is one whole small chicken per person — the traditional sam-bok serving. The glutinous rice cooks inside into a porridge, and the ginseng root is the dash of medicine at the heart of it.","delta":{},"ingredients":"1 small whole chicken (600-800g) · 60g glutinous rice, soaked · 1 ginseng root · 4 garlic cloves · 3 jujube (dates) · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1L water · salt & pepper","method":["Soak the rice 30 minutes; it doubles as it cooks.","Stuff the cavity two-thirds full: rice, ginseng, garlic, jujube, ginger. Truss closed.","Cover with cold water, bring to a boil, skim thoroughly.","Simmer at a bare tremble 1 h 15 min, turning once, until the broth turns milky white.","Season with salt and pepper at the table only; eat the chicken, the porridge, then the broth."]},{"name":"Budget — Samgyetang for Two from One Bird","icon":"💰","costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"One big bird, two bowls, the same milky ginseng broth — the practical family version of a dish that is traditionally one-chicken-each.","trivia":"Feeding two from one larger bird is the honest home version — the broth and the stuffed-rice porridge stretch perfectly, and a pot of rice on the side closes the gap.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use one larger chicken (about 1.2kg) for two servings. Same stuffing, same gentle simmer, but add an extra 20-30 minutes for the bigger bird. Serve the shredded chicken and porridge into two bowls over steamed rice, and pour the milky broth over both — the broth is easy to share, and the pot of rice makes it a full meal for two."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Samgyetang in the Pressure Cooker","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The same milky restorative in 40 minutes — pressure cooking does in minutes what the stove does in an hour, and the broth is honestly just as milky.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely works for samgyetang: the chicken and rice cook together in about 35-40 minutes, and the high pressure emulsifies the collagen just as well as a long open simmer. It is the modern Korean home shortcut for a school-night tonic.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Stuff and truss the chicken exactly as the classic, then pressure-cook on high for 30-35 minutes with the water and aromatics, following the cooker's natural release. The broth comes out milky and the chicken fall-apart — add the spring onion and a minute on the sauté setting to finish, then serve exactly as the classic with salt and pepper at the table."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-galbi-jjim","name":"Galbi-jjim","nameAlt":"Korean braised short ribs — the party centrepiece that tastes like a hug","aliases":["galbi jjim","braised short ribs","korean braised beef ribs","kalbi jjim"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","braise","celebration","party","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","party","family","special","new-year"],"mood":"Celebratory & comforting","time":"Prep 25 min · Braise 1 h 45 min","health":"A special-occasion rich dish — the sauce is sweet and the ribs rich, but the long braise renders much of the fat away. This is the celebration plate, not the daily one.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · pear (the tenderiser)","budget":"★☆☆ — short ribs are the special-tier cut; everything else is pantry","audience":"The celebration centrepiece — served at holidays, birthdays and the big family Sunday table, where it sits next to the galbi on the grill as the slow answer to the fast.","season":"Any special gathering; the holidays and cold-season Sunday tables are its natural home","ingredients":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher) · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear or apple juice (the quiet tenderiser) · 20ml mirin or Korean cooking wine · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 1 potato (about 120g), cubed · 1 carrot, in thick chunks · 5 shiitake mushrooms (optional) · 6 chestnuts (optional, traditional) · 100ml water or stock · black pepper · spring onion & sesame seeds for the finish","method":"⚖️ GALBI-JJIM IS THE SLOW SIBLING OF GALBI — where galbi is the fast, caramelised grill, galbi-jjim is the long, glossy braise, and together they are the twin pillars of a Korean celebration table. 'Jjim' means braised or steamed, and this is the dish served when the table must impress: short ribs cooked until they are falling-apart tender in a sweet-salty soy glaze, with potato and carrot soaking it all up. THE TWO JOBS ARE THE SOAK AND THE BRAISE.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE SOAK — THE QUIET STEP THAT MAKES IT GREAT. Put the ribs in a bowl of cold water for 30 minutes to draw out the blood (this is the traditional Korean step that keeps the finished glaze clean and bright instead of dark and murky), then drain. It takes no effort and changes everything.\n\nBLANCH THE RIBS, THEN THE SAUCE ALL AT ONCE. Bring a pot of water to a boil, add the ribs for 5 minutes, and skim the scum that rises — drain and rinse the ribs clean. Whisk the soy, pear juice, mirin, honey, sesame oil, garlic and ginger into a glossy sauce. The pear does what it always does: tenderises gently and gives the glaze its deep caramel sweetness.\n\n⚖️ THE BRAISE — LOW, SLOW, AND THE REDUCTION IS THE SAUCE. Put the ribs in a pot, pour the sauce and the water (or stock) over, and bring to a gentle bubble. Then simmer LOW, covered, for about 1 hour 15 minutes — the ribs are nearly done when the meat is beginning to pull away from the bone. ADD THE POTATO, CARROT, MUSHROOMS AND CHESTNUTS, and simmer 20-25 minutes more, until the vegetables are tender and the ribs are truly falling-apart.\n\n⚖️ THE FINISH — REDUCE THE SAUCE TO A GLAZE. Lift the ribs and vegetables out, then boil the remaining liquid hard for 5-8 minutes until it thickens into a glossy, lacquer-like sauce — this reduction is the entire difference between a braise and a glaze. Pour it over the ribs, scatter the spring onion and sesame seeds, and serve with steamed rice.\n\nTHE RIBS SHOULD BE SO TENDER THAT A CHOPSTICK SLICES THEM. That is the sole test of a properly done galbi-jjim.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Braise 1 h 45 min","kcal":"~580 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 32g · Carbs 38g · Fat 32g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Galbi-jjim is famously BETTER on day two — the ribs drink the glaze and the sauce deepens. Keeps 4 days in the fridge, reheated gently. Freezes beautifully for a month, in its sauce. The potatoes soften further, so add fresh ones when reheating a frozen portion.","chefNotes":"Three rules: the cold-water SOAK draws the blood that keeps the glaze bright, the braise must be LOW and patient (chopstick-through is the test), and the final REDUCTION is what turns it from a braise into a glossy centrepiece. Do not skip the reduction — it is the whole shine.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (non-negotiable) · Kimchi · A fresh cucumber side · Cold soju or beer","goesWith":["Galbi","Samgyeopsal","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Galbi-jjim is the slow-cooked sibling in the Korean barbecue family: where galbi is grilled fast over coals, jjim braises low and long, and Korean households typically serve both at a celebration table — the fast and the slow, the charred and the melt. It is a staple of the Korean holiday table, and in the southern tradition chestnuts are the signature addition. The name is the method: jjim means steamed or braised, and the dish is proof of how patient Korean cooking can be when it wants a bone to become silk.","howThisFeels":"A glossy lacquered pile of ribs that surrender at the touch of chopsticks, the sweet-salty glaze clinging, the potato soaked in everything — the dish that makes the whole table go quiet mid-bite and then start planning seconds aloud.","leftovers":["The day-two move: the glazed ribs over steaming rice with a fried egg — the humble bibimbap of a celebration dish.","Use the leftover glaze as a stir-fry sauce — a spoon with any quick vegetables turns them Korean in a minute.","Freeze the ribs in their sauce for a month; reheat gently and add fresh potato and carrot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Galbi-jjim with Potato & Chestnut","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":56,"howThisFeels":"The full celebration braise — falling-apart ribs, glossy soy-pear glaze, potato, carrot, mushroom and chestnut.","trivia":"The classic is the holiday version — short ribs braised with chestnuts (the southern tradition), potato, carrot and shiitake, finished with a glossy reduction.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g short ribs · 40ml soy · 20ml pear juice · 20ml mirin · 15ml honey · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 120g potato · 1 carrot · 5 shiitake · 6 chestnuts · 100ml water · pepper · spring onion · sesame seeds","method":["Soak the ribs in cold water 30 minutes; drain. Blanch 5 minutes; skim and rinse.","Whisk soy, pear, mirin, honey, sesame, garlic, ginger. Put ribs, sauce, water in a pot; simmer LOW, covered, 1 h 15 min.","Add potato, carrot, shiitake, chestnuts; simmer 20-25 min until everything is tender and ribs fall apart.","Lift out the solids; boil the liquid hard 5-8 min until it reduces to a glossy glaze.","Pour the glaze over; finish with spring onion and sesame. Serve with rice."]},{"name":"Chicken Galbi-Jjim (Budget)","icon":"🐔","costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy braise with chicken drumsticks — a quarter of the cost, all of the comfort.","trivia":"Chicken legs take the same glaze beautifully and cook in half the time — a legitimate budget fork that Korean families love.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher)","to":"400g chicken drumsticks or thighs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the 30-minute soak (chicken has no blood to draw). Braise the chicken in the same sauce at a gentle bubble for 35-40 minutes until fall-apart, then add the vegetables for the last 15 minutes. Reduce the sauce exactly as the classic — the glaze clings to chicken just as lovingly."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Pressure-Cooker Galbi-Jjim","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy lacquer in 45 minutes — the pressure cooker does the braise in a quarter of the time, and the reduction still delivers the shine.","trivia":"A pressure cooker transforms galbi-jjim: the ribs reach falling-apart in about 30 minutes. The final reduction is the step that must not be skipped — it is what takes the dish from stew to celebration.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Soak and blanch as the classic, then pressure-cook the ribs with the sauce and water on high for 25-30 minutes, natural release. Add the vegetables and cook a further 5 minutes. Then lift everything out and reduce the liquid hard on the stovetop to the glossy glaze — the reduction is non-negotiable, even in the fast lane."}]}},{"name":"Mushroom & Daikon Galbi-Jjim (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The glossy braise, given over to king oyster mushrooms and daikon — and the daikon does its famous trick of tasting like the sauce itself.","trivia":"Daikon radish braised in soy-sweet sauce is a beloved Korean banchan-adjacent classic — it drinks the glaze and becomes startlingly savoury. King oyster mushrooms, thick-sliced, give the closest thing to a meaty bite.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g king oyster mushrooms, thick-sliced"},{"item":"200g daikon radish, in thick chunks"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the soak and blanch. Simmer the daikon in the sauce and water for 20 minutes first (it needs the head start), then add the king oyster mushrooms, potato and carrot, and simmer 20 minutes. Reduce the glaze exactly as the classic. The mushrooms and daikon drink the soy-pear glaze and carry the celebration plate with honour."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kongguksu","name":"Kongguksu","nameAlt":"Cold soybean noodle soup — summer's cooling bowl of pureed beans","aliases":["kongguksu","cold soybean soup","soybean noodle soup","korean soybean noodles"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","cold","summer","vegan","light"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["summer","everyday","lunch","refresh"],"mood":"Cooling & refreshing","time":"Prep 15 min (+soak overnight) · Cook 25 min","health":"One of the healthiest bowls in Korean cooking — pureed soybeans are a protein-and-fibre powerhouse, and the dish is naturally vegan.","allergen":"Soy (the soybeans) · wheat (the noodles)","budget":"★★★ — dried soybeans are cheap; this is the most affordable summer peak in Korean cooking","audience":"The summer-cooling bowl — the dairy-free, protein-dense answer to a milkshake, served as a meal.","season":"SUMMER ONLY, and proudly so — kongguksu is eaten when the thermometer gives up, and its fans mourn it in winter","ingredients":"100g dried soybeans (about 1 generous handful) · 120g thin wheat noodles (somyeon / thin spaghetti works) · 5ml salt · 5g sugar (just a touch) · 5ml sesame oil · toasted sesame seeds · 1 slice cucumber, in fine matchsticks · 1 slice tomato (the classic garnish) · a few ice cubes · salt for the table","method":"⚖️ KONGGUKSU IS SUMMER IN A BOWL — cold, milky, quietly sweet: pureed soybeans as the soup, thin wheat noodles swimming through it, and a bright cucumber-and-tomato garnish. It is Korea's dairy-free, protein-dense answer to a cold milkshake, and its whole character is one of pure, cooling simplicity. The dish is naturally vegan, one of the few Korean bowls that needs no adaptation.\n\nTHE BEANS — SOAKED OVERNIGHT, THEN TRANSFORMED. This is the day-before part, and it is the whole dish: dried soybeans soak in cold water overnight (at least 8 hours), then are drained, boiled in fresh water 5 minutes, drained, and the skins slipped off by hand or by rubbing them in water — removing the skins is what makes the soup SILKY instead of gritty. This is the classic home method; the honest shortcut is to use 300ml of good unsweetened soya milk plus a handful of cooked beans, blended to boost the milky body.\n\n⚖️ THE BLEND — SLOW, LOTS OF COLD WATER, AND THE STRAIN. Blend the skinned beans with cold water (about 2 cups for the batch), starting slow and working up, until it is completely smooth. Then strain it through a fine sieve or cheesecloth, pressing the pulp hard to extract every drop — then squeeze the pulp once more. The result is a milky, nutty, gently sweet liquid that looks like dairy and tastes like beans in the best possible way. Season it with salt and just a touch of sugar, and chill it thoroughly — ice-cold is not optional.\n\nTHE NOODLES — COOKED, THEN SHOCKED. Cook the thin noodles per the packet, then the indispensably Korean step: rinse them under cold running water, rub them gently, drain, and give them a final swish in iced water. Cold, slippery, separate strands — a warm clump would sink the whole bowl. Drain very well.\n\n⚖️ BUILD THE BOWL — NOODLES BELOW, MILKY SOUP OVER, GARNISH BRIGHT. Coil the cold noodles in the bowl, pour the icy soybean soup over and around, and crown with cucumber matchsticks, a slice of tomato, sesame seeds and a thread of sesame oil. Add an ice cube or two if the day demands it. Serve with kimchi on the side — the sharp fermented spice cutting against the milky sweetness is the pairing the dish was born for.\n\nEAT IT FAST, like the other cold-noodle dishes of Korean summer. Season at the table — every Korean table keeps salt beside the kongguksu, because the soup's seasoning is meant to be tuned by the eater.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min (+soak overnight) · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g · Carbs 62g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~500mg","storage":"The soybean soup itself keeps 2 days refrigerated in a jar — shake before use. The noodles must be freshly cooked and iced; they clump and dull overnight. The dish is a same-day bowl, best eaten at its icy peak.","chefNotes":"The three rules: SOAK the beans overnight and remove the skins (silky, not gritty), STRAIN the blend through a sieve pressing the pulp (the milk is in the squeeze), and RINSE the noodles ice-cold so each strand stays separate. Season at the table — salt is the eater's, not the cook's.","pairsWith":"Kimchi (the essential companion — sharp fire against the milky sweet) · A cold, crisp cucumber side · Chilled barley tea","goesWith":["Naengmyeon","Gimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kongguksu is one of Korean summer's most personal foods: the milky puree of soybeans is so plain-looking that newcomers often underestimate it, and so beloved that Koreans argue about the details — whole-skin vs skinned, strained vs coarse, sesame oil or not. It hails from the northern tradition, where the soybean was the everyday protein, and it remains the dish that proves a bowl can be almost nothing — cold beans, cold noodles, salt — and still be one of the great refreshments of the Korean table.","howThisFeels":"The first shock of milky-cold soybean soup against warm summer air, the slippery tangle of noodles, the bright crunch of cucumber — and the quiet satisfaction of a bowl that is both a meal and a milkshake.","leftovers":["The soybean liquid keeps 2 days cold — shake well and it is ready for a fresh bowl of noodles the next day.","Stir the leftover soy milk through the next morning's porridge (or add to a bowl of museli) for a Korean-strength start.","The squeezed bean pulp is not waste: toast it dry in a pan with a little sesame and it becomes a crunchy, protein-dense garnish (kong-gom)."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Skinned-Bean Kongguksu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — beans soaked overnight, skinned by hand, blended slow and strained silky, over iced noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the home method: dried soybeans soaked overnight, skins slipped off by hand, blended with cold water and strained through a sieve until silky. The skin-removal is the difference between milky and gritty.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g dried soybeans · 120g thin wheat noodles · 5ml salt · 5g sugar · 5ml sesame oil · sesame seeds · cucumber · tomato · ice cubes","method":["Soak the soybeans in cold water overnight (8+ hours).","Boil 5 minutes, drain, slip off the skins by rubbing in water.","Blend with cold water until completely smooth; strain through a sieve, pressing the pulp for every drop.","Season with salt and a touch of sugar; chill thoroughly until icy.","Cook the noodles, rinse under cold water, swish in iced water, drain well.","Coil noodles in a bowl, pour the icy bean soup over, garnish with cucumber, tomato, sesame. Serve with kimchi."]},{"name":"Quick — Soy-Milk Kongguksu (15 Minutes)","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The honest weeknight shortcut — good unsweetened soya milk boosted with blended cooked beans, no overnight soak and no skin-slipping.","trivia":"A quality unsweetened soya milk plus a handful of cooked (tinned) soybeans, blended together, is a legitimate shortcut — the tinned beans give the milky body and the soya milk gives the base. The noodles and the ice-cold ritual carry the rest.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Blend 300ml unsweetened soya milk with a drained handful of tinned soybeans (about 60g) and a pinch of salt until completely smooth — taste and adjust with more milk for silkier. Stick it in the freezer while the noodles cook, then chill the noodles in iced water and build the bowl exactly as the classic. Fifteen minutes, all of the refreshment."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-budae-jjigae","name":"Budae Jjigae","nameAlt":"Army stew — the extraordinary Korean War fusion born of scarcity","aliases":["budae jjigae","army stew","korean army stew","부대찌개"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","fusion","kimchi","one-pot","comfort","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","comfort","winter","sharing","leftover"],"mood":"Bold, fun & communal","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 20 min","health":"Not health food — this is a fun, indulgent fusion pot built on processed meats, noodles and cheese. It is a comfort blowout, eaten socially.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · gluten (noodles, some sausages) · dairy (the cheese) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — tinned meats are among the cheapest proteins around; this stew is built to feed a crowd for almost nothing","audience":"The communal feast — a bubbling pot in the middle of the table that everyone dips into, built for sharing, gaming nights and cold evenings.","season":"Winter pot-meals and late-night gatherings — but its fans will fire it up any night the craving strikes","ingredients":"100g old kimchi, chopped · 50g SPAM or corned meat, sliced (classic) · 2 hot dog sausages, sliced (classic) · 30g baked beans (the classic American-ration nod) · 100g firm tofu, cubed · 1 packet instant noodles (ramyun noodles — the classic finish) · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 1 tsp minced garlic · 10ml soy sauce · 300ml water or chicken stock · 1 slice processed cheese (American-style — the final flourish) · 2 spring onions · black pepper","method":"⚖️ BUDAE JJIGAE IS THE EXTRAORDINARY FUSION THAT SCARCITY BUILT — a bubbling Korean pot of kimchi, SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, instant noodles and melted cheese, born after the Korean War when Koreans living near US army bases combined the leftover American ration tins with the Korean ingredients they had always used. The result, named 'army stew' (budae jjigae), became one of Korea's most beloved comfort foods and a genuine cultural artefact. It is not haute cuisine — it is the glorious opposite: a communal pot that is unapologetically tinned, processed and joyful.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE KOREAN FUNDAMENT: SEAR THE KIMCHI. Everything that is good about this stew starts exactly like kimchi jjigae: chop the old kimchi, and fry it hard in a little oil until its edges darken and the whole kitchen smells of fermented chilli. This is the step that gives the pot its deep, tangy, savoury base.\n\nBUILD THE POT, LAYER BY LAYER. Spread the seared kimchi across the bottom of a wide pot. Arrange the sliced SPAM, hot dogs, tofu, baked beans and any vegetables in neat piles around the edge — the laid-out look is part of the dish's charm, a mosaic of rations and Korean pantry that bubbles together as it cooks.\n\n⚖️ THE SAUCE IS A SPICY MIXTURE, ACTIVE IN THE CENTRE. Into the middle goes the sauce: gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, soy and a splash of the stock, whisked into a red paste. Pour the rest of the stock (or water) around, so the vegetables still peek above the liquid. Bring the whole pot to a furious bubble.\n\nNOW THE ANTHEMIC FINISH — THE NOODLES AND THE CHEESE. When the pot is bubbling hard, lay the instant noodle block in the centre, submerging it gently into the broth, and place the slice of processed cheese on top. As the noodles soften and the cheese melts into a molten island of orange, the pot is ready. Turn the heat off just before the cheese fully vanishes — the half-melted cheese pooling on the noodles is the money shot.\n\n⚖️ SERVE FROM THE POT, EVERYONE DIPS IN. Budae jjigae is eaten communally — everyone reaches in with their chopsticks, fishing out noodles and SPAM and tofu until the broth is nearly gone. The last move, the classic one, is to break some rice into the remaining spicy broth and eat it as a little congee — nothing is wasted.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 54g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~1600mg","storage":"The broth is gold — keep it a day and reheat with fresh noodles and cheese for an equally glorious round two. The stew itself does not freeze well (the noodles and cheese collapse); the seared-kimchi-and-sauce base freezes for a month.","chefNotes":"Two rules: SEAR the kimchi first (the whole base is in that browning), and WAIT for the noodles and cheese until the pot is already boiling hard — they are the finish, not the beginning. Lay everything out in piles before the liquid goes in; the mosaic is part of the charm.","pairsWith":"Cold beer (the definitive companion) · Steamed rice to finish the broth · Kimchi on the side, always","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Tteokbokki","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Budae jjigae's story is the reason it is extraordinary: after the Korean War, Koreans in Uijeongbu and near US bases found themselves with American military ration cans — SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, processed cheese — an irony of hunger turned into invention. Korean cooks married those tins to the pantry they had always used: kimchi, gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, instant noodles. The fusion was so beloved it became a national comfort food, and today 'army stew' is served everywhere from two-dollar street joints to high-end reinterpretations — a dish that turns the memory of scarcity into a feast.","howThisFeels":"The whole pot arriving bubbling, the molten cheese island melting over the noodles, everyone reaching in at once — salty, spicy, tinned, joyful, and somehow deeply Korean.","leftovers":["The broth is the treasure: reheat it and add a fresh noodle block and cheese for round two.","Break cooked rice into the leftover broth and simmer 2 minutes for a spicy congee finish.","Freeze the seared-kimchi-and-sauce base for a month; a fresh pot is then 10 minutes away."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Army Stew (SPAM & Hot Dogs)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The original fusion — SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, kimchi, instant noodles and a molten cheese island.","trivia":"The classic is the post-war original: SPAM and hot dogs layered with kimchi and gochugaru, finished with instant noodles and one slice of processed cheese — the exact configuration that won the nation.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi · 50g SPAM, sliced · 2 hot dogs, sliced · 30g baked beans · 100g tofu, cubed · 1 packet instant noodles · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 1 tsp garlic · 10ml soy · 300ml stock · 1 slice processed cheese · 2 spring onions · pepper","method":["Sear the chopped old kimchi hard until its edges darken.","Arrange SPAM, hot dogs, tofu and baked beans in piles around the seared kimchi in a wide pot.","Whisk gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, soy and a splash of stock into a sauce in the centre; pour the rest of the stock around.","Bring to a furious bubble; lay the instant noodle block in and the cheese slice on top.","Eat communally from the pot as the cheese melts; finish with rice broken into the remaining broth."]},{"name":"Budget — Beans-Forward Army Stew (Vegan-friendly)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The same bubbly fusion pot with the tinned meat scaled right back — more beans, more mushrooms, more tofu, all the flavour.","trivia":"The tinned meat is the flavour-carrying luxury, not the essence — a budget pot with extra baked beans, mushrooms and tofu keeps the whole spirit of the dish, and removing the SPAM and hot dogs entirely makes it genuinely vegan (gochujang and kimchi are naturally plant-based).","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Double the baked beans, add a handful of sliced mushrooms and an extra block of tofu, and halve (or drop entirely, for vegan) the SPAM and hot dogs. The seared kimchi and the spicy sauce do all the work — the bright, bubbling, communal soul of the pot survives completely. Use vegetable stock for the vegan version."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 10-Minute Single-Pot Budae","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The solo or couple's version — one small pot, everything tossed in together, noodles and cheese on top the instant it bubbles.","trivia":"The quick version simply skips the neat mosaic layering: everything meets in one small pot, the sauce whisked right in, and the noodle-and-cheese finish is unchanged. The seared kimchi still leads — that step is the non-negotiable.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"For one small practical pot, sear the kimchi in a deep saucepan, toss in the tinned and cubed ingredients and the whisked sauce, pour the stock over, and bring to a boil. The instant it bubbles hard, drop in the noodle block and cheese. Single-pot, 10 minutes, the same molten finish."}]}}]},
];